window.CASES=window.CASES||[];
window.CASES.push(
{ id:"fcp34", kind:"case", title:"The assistant that forgot the old manuals", level:4, domains:["p34"],
  context:"You built and run a retrieval-augmented assistant for a regional fiber contractor. Crews ask it about splice-closure procedures, OTDR acceptance limits and OLT runbooks from the truck. The stack: PostgreSQL with pgvector holding 10,148 chunks, hybrid retrieval (Postgres full-text keyword ranking plus vector search, top 20 each, fused with reciprocal rank fusion, k = 60), the top 8 fused candidates go to a cross-encoder reranker, and the best 5 go to the model with numbered citations. On Thursday, Oct 22, a teammate switched the embedding model from bge-large-en-v1.5 to bge-m3 (both 1,024 dimensions) to support a Spanish-speaking crew. Since then crews keep getting 'not in the provided documents' for procedures they know exist, and the nightly eval has turned red. The contractor's QA lead wants it fixed before Monday's ring-acceptance work.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Nightly retrieval and answer eval (evals/field_rag.yaml, 60 cases)", text:"cases: 48 about documents ingested before 2026-10-22, 12 about documents ingested after\n\nrun         prompt  embed_model         recall@5  exact_match  wrong_refusals\n2026-10-21  p-v14   bge-large-en-v1.5   55/60     52/60        1\n2026-10-25  p-v14   bge-m3              25/60     23/60        29\n\nbreakdown of the 2026-10-25 run                          recall@5\n  questions about pre-10-22 documents                    13/48\n  questions about post-10-22 documents                   12/12\n\npre-10-22 hits whose question contains an exact alarm code or part number:    12 of 13\npre-10-22 misses whose question contains an exact alarm code or part number:   2 of 35"},
    {label:"psql: what the chunks table actually holds", text:"rag=# SELECT embed_model, count(*), min(embedded_at)::date AS first, max(embedded_at)::date AS last\nrag-#   FROM chunks GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;\n    embed_model    | count |   first    |    last\n-------------------+-------+------------+------------\n bge-large-en-v1.5 |  9412 | 2026-03-02 | 2026-10-22\n bge-m3            |   736 | 2026-10-22 | 2026-10-26\n(2 rows)\n\nrag=# \\d chunks   -- abridged\n embedding   | vector(1024)\n embed_model | text\n content_sha | text\nIndexes: \"chunks_embedding_hnsw\" hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)\n\n-- probe: chunk c-04471 (OTDR acceptance table), stored vector vs a fresh bge-m3 embedding of the same text\nrag=# SELECT round((1 - (embedding <=> :'fresh_m3'))::numeric, 4) AS cos_sim FROM chunks WHERE id = 'c-04471';\n cos_sim\n---------\n  0.0213"},
    {label:"Trace of one failing question", text:"trace 9c1e  q=\"how much loss is too much on a fusion joint for ring acceptance?\"\nretrieval.vector (query embedded with bge-m3), top 5:\n  1  c-10233  sim=0.441  newsletter-2026-10.md     [bge-m3]\n  2  c-10198  sim=0.429  heat-safety-memo.md       [bge-m3]\n  3  c-10317  sim=0.418  crew-roster-oct.md        [bge-m3]\n  4  c-10201  sim=0.402  ladder-inspection.md      [bge-m3]\n  5  c-10260  sim=0.399  vehicle-checklist.md      [bge-m3]\nretrieval.keyword, top 3: c-02218 'fusion splicer cleaning' | c-07730 'ring cutover' | c-04490 'closure prep'\ngold chunk c-04471 'Acceptance: fusion splice loss <= 0.10 dB average, <= 0.15 dB max'  [bge-large-en-v1.5]\n  vector rank 2,904 (sim 0.038)   keyword rank 27 (no match on 'joint' or 'too much')\nreranker input: 8 fused candidates, gold chunk not among them\nanswer: \"This is not in the provided documents.\""},
    {label:"Config change and the eval runner's log", text:"# config.yaml, commit 4be1a07, 2026-10-22 15:40\n-embedding_model: bge-large-en-v1.5\n+embedding_model: bge-m3            # used by the ingest worker AND the query service\n reindex: incremental               # re-embed only documents whose content_sha changed\n\n2026-10-25 02:10:03 nightly-eval INFO  starting 60 cases (generator: hosted LLM, judge: hosted LLM)\n2026-10-25 02:11:47 nightly-eval WARN  HTTP 429 Too Many Requests from LLM API, retry-after 2 s (attempt 1/5)\n2026-10-25 02:11:49 nightly-eval INFO  retry ok (case 31)\n2026-10-25 02:12:30 nightly-eval WARN  HTTP 429 Too Many Requests from LLM API, retry-after 2 s (attempt 1/5)\n2026-10-25 02:12:32 nightly-eval INFO  retry ok (case 44)\n2026-10-25 02:14:05 nightly-eval INFO  finished 60/60 cases, 0 errors, p95 latency 3.1 s"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp34q1", q:"Questions about old documents fail while questions about new documents pass. What is the mechanism?", options:["The HNSW index went stale when 736 rows were added and has to be rebuilt with REINDEX","bge-m3 is weaker than bge-large on English text, so retrieval got worse across the board","Queries are now embedded with bge-m3, but 9,412 chunks still hold bge-large-en-v1.5 vectors. The two models' spaces are unrelated, so the cosine between a bge-m3 query and an old chunk is noise near zero, and old chunks can no longer rank on the vector side","Both models output 1,024 dimensions, so the vectors are compatible; the drop must come from a chunking change"], answer:2, why:"Matching dimensions only means pgvector accepts the rows. Each model's coordinates mean something different, which the probe shows directly: the same OTDR text scores 0.02 across models. New-document questions pass 12/12, which rules out 'bge-m3 is worse'. HNSW indexes update incrementally, and a stale index would hurt old and new chunks alike."},
    {id:"fcp34q2", q:"Why do 12 of the 13 old-document questions that still pass contain an exact alarm code or part number?", options:["The keyword half of hybrid search ranks the stored text, not the vectors, so it still finds literal tokens such as an alarm code, and RRF carries those chunks to the reranker. Paraphrased questions depend on the vector half, which is broken for old chunks","The reranker has memorized part numbers from earlier traffic","The model knows those codes from its training data and answers without retrieval","Questions with part numbers are shorter, so their embeddings are more accurate"], answer:0, why:"recall@5 measures retrieval, so the model's own knowledge cannot explain it. Keyword ranking is independent of the embedding model, which is exactly why hybrid search kept part of the system alive and hid how badly the vector side had failed. The trace shows the other case: a paraphrased question ('fusion joint', 'too much') gives the keyword engine nothing to match."},
    {id:"fcp34q3", q:"How should you treat the HTTP 429 warnings in the eval runner's log?", options:["As the cause: the rate-limited calls returned empty context and the model refused","As the first thing to fix: raise the API rate limit and rerun before investigating anything else","As the cause of the wrong refusals, because retried calls use a different temperature","As irrelevant: both calls were retried successfully, the run finished 60/60 with 0 errors, and recall@5 is measured on retrieval before any model call"], answer:3, why:"A 429 is backpressure from the API. Here it was handled correctly by retry-after, and it happens downstream of retrieval anyway. The collapse is visible in recall@5, a number computed before generation, so no generator-side event can explain it."},
    {id:"fcp34q4", q:"Which plan fixes the assistant safely?", options:["Lower the similarity threshold and raise vector top-k from 20 to 100 so old chunks get through","Restore service now by pointing the query embedder back to bge-large-en-v1.5 and re-embedding the 736 new chunks with it; then embed all 10,148 chunks with bge-m3 into a new column or index, run the eval against it, and switch queries over only when recall@5 matches the old baseline","Fine-tune bge-m3 on the old chunks so its vectors line up with bge-large","Delete the 736 bge-m3 rows, since the old index was fine"], answer:1, why:"The rollback is immediate and puts query and index in one space. The rebuild-then-switch approach (blue-green for indexes) moves to the new model only after the eval proves it. A larger top-k only ranks more noise. Fine-tuning cannot make two independent models share coordinates. Deleting the new rows leaves bge-m3 queries searching bge-large vectors, which breaks everything."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the incident note for the contractor's QA lead: the diagnosis and the evidence that proves it, the immediate mitigation, the permanent fix, how you will verify it, and the guardrails that stop a silent embedding-model change from happening again.",
    rubric:["Identifies the mixed vector spaces: 9,412 chunks embedded with bge-large-en-v1.5 and 736 with bge-m3, queried with bge-m3. Cites the 0.02 probe, the 13/48 versus 12/12 split and the gold chunk at vector rank 2,904.","Explains why some old questions still pass (the keyword half of hybrid search matches exact codes) and why matching 1,024-dimension vectors hid the problem from the database.","Dismisses the 429 warnings with evidence (retried, 60/60, 0 errors, recall measured before generation) and does not blame HNSW, chunking or the prompt.","Mitigates immediately (roll the query model back and re-embed the 736 chunks to match, or filter by embed_model), then fixes permanently with a full re-embed into a separate column or index that is switched over after the eval passes. Notes that the re-embed is small (about 4 million tokens).","Verifies with the eval set: recall@5 back to at least 55/60 overall and on the pre-10-22 subset, wrong refusals at most 1, plus a few hand-checked traces.","Prevents recurrence: stores the model name and version with the index and refuses queries embedded by a different model, treats a model change as a full rebuild, and gates retrieval config changes on the eval in CI."],
    reference:"The index holds two incompatible vector spaces. Since commit 4be1a07 on Oct 22, the query service has embedded every question with bge-m3, but reindex: incremental re-embedded only the 736 chunks whose content changed, so 9,412 chunks still carry bge-large-en-v1.5 vectors. Both models emit 1,024 numbers, so pgvector stored them without complaint, but the numbers mean different things: the same OTDR acceptance text scores a cosine of 0.02 across the two models. Old chunks therefore never rank on the vector side (the splice-loss gold chunk sits at rank 2,904), and the reranker never sees them. The eval shows exactly this pattern: new documents 12/12, old documents 13/48, and 12 of those 13 contain an exact code that keyword search still matches. The 429s were retried and happen after retrieval, so they play no part. Mitigation today: point the query embedder back to bge-large-en-v1.5 and re-embed the 736 new chunks with it. Recall should return to the Oct 21 baseline. Permanent fix: embed all 10,148 chunks (about 4 million tokens, under an hour on our GPU) with bge-m3 into a new column with its own HNSW index, run the 60-case eval against it, and switch over when recall@5 is at least 55/60 with at most one wrong refusal. Prevention: record the embedding model with the index, reject mismatched queries, treat a model change as a rebuild, and run the retrieval eval in CI.",
    alternatives:["Keep bge-large-en-v1.5 for English documents and add bge-m3 as a separate index only for the Spanish material, routing by language. This is valid if routing is reliable, but it doubles what you have to operate and evaluate.","Re-embed everything with bge-m3 right away, with no rollback. This fixes the outage within the hour, but it skips proving that bge-m3 is at least as good on English procedures and leaves no fallback if it is not.","Rebuild the HNSW index or tune ef_search. This is the wrong layer: the vectors themselves are in the wrong space, so the index rebuilds faithfully and nothing improves."]},
  hints:["Split the eval by when the source document was ingested. What changed on Oct 22, and which chunks did it touch?","Two embedding models with the same dimension do not share a vector space. Which model produced the stored vectors, and which one embeds the query?","9,412 chunks still hold bge-large vectors while queries use bge-m3; only the keyword half still finds them. Roll the query model back now, then re-embed everything into a new index and switch after the eval passes."] }
,
{ id:"fcp35", kind:"case", title:"Dark ONTs routed to the remote queue", level:4, domains:["p35"],
  context:"You maintain truckroll-gbm, a gradient-boosted model for a regional fiber ISP. It predicts whether a trouble ticket needs a truck roll (a field visit) or can be fixed remotely. Tickets scored 'truck' go straight to dispatch. The rest wait in the remote queue, where an agent works them within a day. On Oct 12 the team shipped model v7 together with fsvc 2.0, a rewritten online feature service, so features no longer come from the nightly warehouse batch. Two weeks later dispatch reports fewer trucks, but customers with no service at all are sitting in the remote queue for a day or two before anyone sends a tech. Labels (whether a truck was eventually needed) arrive about 72 hours after a ticket closes. A storm is forecast for the weekend, and the operations manager wants a cause and a fix first.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Model monitoring report", text:"truckroll-gbm v7 (deployed 2026-10-12 with fsvc 2.0); v6 used nightly batch features\n\nwindow             model  pred_truck_rate  labeled_n  precision  recall\n2026-09-28..10-11  v6     0.31             4,120      0.78       0.81\n2026-10-12..10-18  v7     0.18             3,960      0.80       0.47\n2026-10-19..10-25  v7     0.17             -- labels pending (72 h delay)\n\nfeature PSI, window 10-19..10-25 vs the training reference\nfeature              importance  PSI    status\nrx_power_dbm         0.38        1.94   ALERT\nrepeat_tickets_30d   0.17        0.29   ALERT\nont_uptime_h         0.12        0.05   ok\nplan_tier            0.04        0.02   ok"},
    {label:"Training pipeline vs the new online feature code", text:"# training: features/batch.sql -> pandas -> scikit-learn Pipeline, saved as model.pkl\n# rx_power_dbm is NULL when the OLT cannot reach the ONT (ONT dark, fiber cut, no power)\npre = ColumnTransformer([(\"rx\", SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\", add_indicator=True), [\"rx_power_dbm\"]), ...])\nmodel = Pipeline([(\"pre\", pre), (\"gbm\", HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=400))])\n\n# serving: fsvc 2.0, app/features.py\ndef build_row(ticket, telem, redis):\n    rx = float(telem.get(\"rx_power_dbm\") or 0.0)     # OLT API returns None for dark ONTs\n    since = time.time() - 30 * 86400\n    repeats = redis.zcount(f\"tickets:{ticket.acct}\", since, \"+inf\")\n    return {\"rx_power_dbm\": rx, \"repeat_tickets_30d\": repeats, ...}\n\n# ticket ingest job, app/ticket_ingest.py\nredis.zadd(f\"tickets:{acct}\", {ticket_id: ts})\nredis.zremrangebyscore(f\"tickets:{acct}\", 0, time.time() - 7 * 86400)   # keep a week"},
    {label:"Prediction store: served rows vs training data", text:"served rows 2026-10-19..10-25 (n = 5,212) vs training set (n = 61,480)\n\nrx_power_dbm            served            training\nNULL                    0      (0.0%)     13,341 (21.7%)\n= 0.0 exactly           1,204  (23.1%)    0\n-8 to 0 dBm             0                 0        (above GPON class B+ overload)\n-28 to -8 dBm           4,008  (76.9%)    48,139 (78.3%)\nmedian of non-null      -19.6             -19.8\n\nrepeat_tickets_30d mean 0.41              1.12\n\nserved tickets with rx = 0.0 that v7 scored 'truck':   61 of 1,204 (5.1%)\nv6 tickets with rx NULL that v6 scored 'truck':        89.6%"},
    {label:"Model container startup log and a replay check", text:"2026-10-12 09:14:02 fsvc-model[1] InconsistentVersionWarning: Trying to unpickle estimator SimpleImputer\n  from version 1.6.1 when using version 1.7.2. This might lead to breaking code or invalid results. Use at your own risk.\n2026-10-12 09:14:02 fsvc-model[1] (same warning for ColumnTransformer, HistGradientBoostingClassifier)\n2026-10-12 09:14:03 fsvc-model[1] model loaded: truckroll-gbm v7 sha256=5e0c41f9...\n\n$ python replay_check.py --rows holdout_500.parquet --endpoint http://fsvc-model:8080/score-raw\nsent 500 raw holdout rows straight to the model endpoint (rx_power_dbm NULL in 109 of them)\nidentical predictions to the offline model: 500/500   max |dp| = 0.0\np99 latency 38 ms (SLO 150 ms)"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp35q1", q:"Precision held at about 0.80 while recall fell from 0.81 to 0.47 and the truck rate fell from 0.31 to 0.18. What does that pattern tell you first?", options:["Concept drift: customers' problems changed in October, so the model has to be retrained","The model stopped flagging a whole group of real truck rolls, while the tickets it still flags are as right as before. A slice of inputs is being scored 'remote', which points to a change in the feature path, not a general loss of skill","The labels are delayed, so the recall figure is not real yet","Someone raised the decision threshold in v7"], answer:1, why:"A higher threshold or a general loss of skill usually moves precision too. Steady precision with collapsing recall means the positives the model still predicts are fine, and a specific group has dropped out. The 10-12..10-18 window is already labeled, so the recall number is real. The deploy date lines up with the feature-service change, not with any change in customers."},
    {id:"fcp35q2", q:"Why is float(telem.get(\"rx_power_dbm\") or 0.0) so damaging for this model?", options:["0.0 dBm means 'no light', which is what a dark ONT has, so the value is right and the model is wrong","It only affects the rare ONTs that really report 0.0 dBm","Float conversion is slow and adds latency","0 dBm is 1 mW, an impossibly strong GPON receive level (overload is about -8 dBm). Dark ONTs, the clearest truck-roll cases, now arrive as 'not missing, excellent signal', so the imputer and missing indicator never fire and the model scores them remote: 5.1% truck under v7 versus 89.6% under v6"], answer:3, why:"dBm is a logarithmic scale referenced to 1 mW, so 'no light' is minus infinity, not zero. Python's `x or 0.0` silently turns None into a valid-looking number, and in training those rows were NULL and flagged by add_indicator=True. 23.1% exact zeros in serving against 21.7% NULLs in training is the fingerprint of that substitution."},
    {id:"fcp35q3", q:"What do the unpickle warning and the replay check tell you together?", options:["Fed raw rows, including 109 with NULL power, the loaded model matches the offline model exactly, so the version warning is not the cause here and the fault is in what fsvc 2.0 feeds the model. Pin scikit-learn anyway","The version mismatch corrupted the imputer; rebuild the container with 1.6.1 and the problem goes away","The replay check is invalid because it bypasses the feature service, so nothing can be concluded","A p99 of 38 ms proves the service is healthy, so the model itself must have degraded"], answer:0, why:"Bypassing the feature service is the point of the check: it isolates one layer. The artifact behaves identically, so the defect sits upstream, in feature construction. The warning is a real maintenance risk worth fixing by pinning versions, but this evidence shows it did not change predictions. Latency says nothing about correctness."},
    {id:"fcp35q4", q:"A teammate proposes retraining v8 on the last 30 days of served features so the model 'learns' that 0.0 means dark. What is the best response?", options:["Agree: retraining on fresh data is the standard response to drift","Agree, but use 60 days of data to get more labels","Decline. Retraining bakes the skew into the model, depends on labels still arriving, and breaks again the day someone fixes the service. Fix the data path instead: pass None through as NaN, compute the 30-day count over 30 days, share one feature definition between training and serving, and until then roll back or route tickets with no ONT reading to dispatch","Decline, and lower the decision threshold until the truck rate returns to 0.31"], answer:2, why:"The model is not stale; its inputs are wrong. Retraining on a bug makes the bug load-bearing. Lowering the threshold raises trucks across every ticket instead of fixing the one slice that is mis-scored, and it would still under-score repeat-ticket customers."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the incident analysis for the operations manager: what is wrong and how the evidence proves it, the mitigation for the next 48 hours, the permanent fix, how you will verify it given the 72-hour label delay, and the monitors and contract checks you will add.",
    rubric:["Identifies the main skew: fsvc 2.0 turns a missing rx_power_dbm (dark ONT) into 0.0 dBm, so the missing indicator never fires. Cites 23.1% exact zeros served versus 21.7% NULL in training, and 5.1% versus 89.6% truck rate for that group.","Identifies the second skew: repeat_tickets_30d counts a Redis set that ingest trims to 7 days (mean 0.41 versus 1.12), and names it a window mismatch.","Uses the replay check to clear the model artifact and the version warning (500/500 identical, NULL rows included), and still recommends pinning scikit-learn.","Mitigates quickly: rolls back to v6 with batch features or routes tickets with no ONT reading straight to dispatch, and re-scores or requeues the open zero-power tickets before the storm.","Fixes permanently with one feature definition for training and serving, NaN passed through, a true 30-day window and a serving parity test in CI, and rejects retraining on the skewed data.","Verifies and monitors: PSI on rx_power_dbm under 0.1 and the truck rate back near 0.31 within days, labeled recall near 0.8 when labels land; adds a contract check (null, or between -40 and -8 dBm) and an alert on the predicted truck rate."],
    reference:"The model is fine; its inputs are not. fsvc 2.0 builds rx_power_dbm with float(telem.get('rx_power_dbm') or 0.0). The OLT returns None for an unreachable ONT. In training those rows were NULL (21.7%) and were imputed with a missing indicator the model learned to read as 'dark ONT, send a truck'. Served data has no NULLs but 23.1% exact zeros, and 0 dBm is 1 mW, far above GPON overload, so dark ONTs look like perfect signal. v7 sends a truck for 5.1% of them, against 89.6% under v6. That explains why recall and the truck rate fell while precision held. A second skew compounds it: repeat_tickets_30d counts a Redis set that ingest trims to 7 days, so its mean fell from 1.12 to 0.41. Replaying 500 raw holdout rows, 109 with NULL power, matched the offline model exactly, which rules out the scikit-learn warning (pin the version anyway). Mitigation today: route every ticket with no ONT reading to dispatch, requeue the 1,204 zero-power tickets still open, and roll back to v6 with batch features. Fix: pass None through as NaN, compute the 30-day count from the ticket table, share one feature definition across training and serving, and add a CI test that replays training rows through fsvc. Verify: PSI under 0.1 and a truck rate near 0.31 this week, then labeled recall near 0.8 after 72 hours. Add a contract check (null or -40 to -8 dBm) and a truck-rate alert. Do not retrain on skewed features.",
    alternatives:["Fix only the None-to-0.0 bug and leave the 7-day window for a later release. This recovers most of the lost recall, because the dark-ONT slice is the biggest error, but repeat-ticket customers stay under-scored. It is acceptable as a first deploy if the window fix is scheduled and tracked.","Move both features into a feature store (Feast or the platform's managed one) with one definition for offline and online. This is the strongest prevention, but it is more than a 48-hour fix, so pair it with the immediate rollback or dispatch rule.","Retrain v8 on served features. This partly 'works' for the zero-power slice, but it bakes the bug into the model, needs labels that are still arriving, and silently breaks when the service is corrected."]},
  hints:["Precision held and recall fell. Which tickets stopped being flagged, and which feature has the highest importance and the worst PSI?","Compare how training and serving represent an unreachable ONT. What does Python's `x or 0.0` do to None, and what does 0 dBm mean on a GPON?","Dark ONTs now arrive as 0.0 dBm instead of missing, so the indicator never fires, and the 30-day count only sees 7 days. Route no-reading tickets to dispatch, pass NaN through, fix the window and add a parity test."] },
{ id:"fcp36", kind:"case", title:"Rows that committed behind the watermark", level:3, domains:["p36","p18"],
  context:"You run the hourly incremental load that copies as-built records (fiber footage placed, splices, OTDR results) from the field app's PostgreSQL database into the contractor's lakehouse, where dbt builds the daily footage report used for invoicing. An Airflow task extracts rows changed since the last watermark and MERGEs them into silver.asbuilt by record_id. The project manager reconciled the lakehouse against the source and found it short: for Oct 21, 86 records and 11,630 ft of placed fiber never arrived, and smaller gaps show up on other days. Every Airflow run is green and no dbt test fails. Invoices for the week go out Wednesday. Find where the rows went, recover them and make the load correct.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Reconciliation: source vs lakehouse by work date", text:"work_date   src_rows  lake_rows  missing  missing_ft\n2026-10-19  1,140     1,140      0        0\n2026-10-20  1,207     1,165      42       5,280\n2026-10-21  1,284     1,198      86       11,630\n2026-10-22  1,251     1,251      0        0\n2026-10-23  1,318     1,279      39       4,410\n2026-10-24  412       412        0        0\n\nmissing rows by writer:  tablet-sync 167   web-entry 0"},
    {label:"The missing Oct 21 rows, and the sync service's log", text:"src=# SELECT updated_at, count(*), min(record_id), max(record_id)\nsrc-#   FROM asbuilt WHERE record_id IN (SELECT record_id FROM missing_2026_10_21) GROUP BY 1;\n          updated_at           | count |  min   |  max\n-------------------------------+-------+--------+--------\n 2026-10-21 01:52:07.413261+00 |    86 | 774102 | 774187\n(1 row)\n\n2026-10-21T01:52:07.409Z tablet-sync BEGIN batch 5521 (crew 17, 86 records)\n2026-10-21T01:52:07.415Z tablet-sync INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (record_id) DO UPDATE SET ..., updated_at = now()\n2026-10-21T01:52:07.602Z tablet-sync uploading 412 photos to object storage (transaction still open)\n2026-10-21T02:19:51.330Z tablet-sync COMMIT batch 5521"},
    {label:"Extract task code and its log", text:"# dags/asbuilt_incremental.py, extract task (abridged); connects to pg-primary\nwm = get_watermark(\"asbuilt\")                 # last max(updated_at) loaded\nrows = pg.fetch(\"SELECT * FROM asbuilt WHERE updated_at > %(wm)s ORDER BY updated_at\", wm=wm)\nmerge_into_silver(rows, key=\"record_id\")      # idempotent MERGE\nset_watermark(\"asbuilt\", max(r.updated_at for r in rows))\n\n[2026-10-21T02:05:02Z] extract: watermark in  = 2026-10-21T01:04:58.120Z\n[2026-10-21T02:05:04Z] extract: fetched 37 rows, max(updated_at) = 2026-10-21T02:04:51.884Z\n[2026-10-21T02:05:04Z] extract: watermark out = 2026-10-21T02:04:51.884Z\n[2026-10-21T03:05:03Z] extract: watermark in  = 2026-10-21T02:04:51.884Z, fetched 22 rows\n[2026-10-21T03:05:05Z] Task exited with return code 0"},
    {label:"PostgreSQL log and pg_stat_activity on the primary", text:"2026-10-21 01:58:12 UTC [2231] LOG:  checkpoints are occurring too frequently (27 seconds apart)\n2026-10-21 01:58:12 UTC [2231] HINT:  Consider increasing the configuration parameter \"max_wal_size\".\n\nsrc=# SELECT pid, application_name, state, xact_start, query_start FROM pg_stat_activity\nsrc-#   WHERE datname = 'src' AND state <> 'idle';      -- sampled at 02:05:02\n pid  | application_name |        state        |       xact_start        |       query_start\n------+------------------+---------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------\n 4471 | tablet-sync      | idle in transaction | 2026-10-21 01:52:07.409 | 2026-10-21 02:04:58.230\n 5120 | airflow-extract  | active              | 2026-10-21 02:05:02.611 | 2026-10-21 02:05:02.611\n(2 rows)"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp36q1", q:"All 86 missing rows carry updated_at = 01:52:07.413, although the batch committed at 02:19:51. Why?", options:["The tablets' clocks were wrong and stamped every record with the same time","In PostgreSQL now() returns the start time of the current transaction, and the sync service wrote all 86 rows in one transaction that stayed open for 27 minutes while it uploaded photos","The lakehouse MERGE overwrote updated_at with the batch time","ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE always stamps the earliest timestamp in a batch"], answer:1, why:"now() (like CURRENT_TIMESTAMP) is fixed at transaction start so that one transaction sees one consistent time; clock_timestamp() is the function that advances. The timestamps come from the database, not the tablets, and the query reads the source table, so the MERGE cannot be involved."},
    {id:"fcp36q2", q:"The rows were already in the table at 02:05. Why did that run miss them, and why will no later run ever load them?", options:["They were uncommitted, so MVCC hid them from the extract's snapshot. The run then moved the watermark to 02:04:51, so when the rows became visible at 02:19:51 their updated_at (01:52:07) was already below it, and a query of the form updated_at > watermark can never return them","The extract read a lagging read replica that had not yet replayed the rows","The extract runs at READ UNCOMMITTED, which skips rows locked by another session","The checkpoint at 01:58 flushed the rows out of the WAL before the extract could read them"], answer:0, why:"The task connects to the primary, so replica lag does not apply. PostgreSQL treats READ UNCOMMITTED as READ COMMITTED; no session ever sees another's uncommitted rows. Checkpoints write dirty pages to disk and recycle old WAL, and they never remove committed or in-flight data. The defect is that the watermark assumes timestamps arrive in commit order, which they do not."},
    {id:"fcp36q3", q:"Which change, on its own, does NOT make the load correct?", options:["Switching to log-based CDC (Debezium on a logical replication slot), which emits changes in commit order","Re-reading a lookback window longer than any transaction may last (enforced with idle_in_transaction_session_timeout) and letting the idempotent MERGE drop the duplicates","Capping each new watermark, minus a small margin, at the start time of the oldest transaction still open when the extract runs","Changing the app to set updated_at = clock_timestamp() instead of now()"], answer:3, why:"clock_timestamp() records when the statement ran, not when the row became visible. A row written at 02:04:50 in a transaction that commits at 02:19 is still invisible at 02:05 and still below the watermark afterwards, so the gap narrows but never closes. The other three handle visibility directly: commit-ordered logs, or windows guaranteed to cover any open transaction."},
    {id:"fcp36q4", q:"How should you treat the 'checkpoints are occurring too frequently' message?", options:["As the root cause: increase max_wal_size and the missing rows will reappear","As proof that WAL was lost, so the missing rows must be restored from backup","As a tuning note about a write burst, likely the big sync batch. It has nothing to do with row visibility or the watermark, and the rows are intact in the source table","As evidence that the primary crashed and the extract read a stale copy"], answer:2, why:"The source query returns all 86 rows, so nothing was lost. The hint is worth a ticket (a larger max_wal_size smooths I/O during bursts), but it describes how dirty pages are flushed, not which rows a snapshot can see."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the post-incident note for the project manager and the data team: the root cause with the evidence chain, how you will recover the 167 missing records before invoicing, the permanent fix, how you will prove the data is complete, and which tests or alerts will catch this class of failure next time.",
    rubric:["Explains the mechanism: now() is the transaction start time, batch 5521 stayed open 27 minutes because photos uploaded inside the transaction, MVCC hid the rows at 02:05, and the watermark moved past their updated_at. Cites the identical timestamps, the idle-in-transaction session and the extract log.","Shows that the Oct 20 and Oct 23 gaps fit the same pattern (tablet-sync only, 167 rows in total) instead of treating Oct 21 as a one-off.","Recovers safely: re-extracts the affected dates and relies on the idempotent MERGE by record_id, reruns dbt for those dates, and confirms the reconciliation shows 0 missing before invoices go out.","Fixes permanently with a commit-ordered method (log-based CDC) or a lookback or safe-watermark design whose margin exceeds a bounded transaction length, and asks the app team to move network I/O out of the transaction.","Adds detection: a daily source-versus-lakehouse reconciliation test (rows and footage per work date) that fails the run, plus an alert on long-open transactions.","Dismisses the checkpoint warning and rejects clock_timestamp() as insufficient, with reasons."],
    reference:"The 167 records were never extracted because the load treats updated_at as if it were commit order. tablet-sync opens a transaction, upserts a crew's batch with updated_at = now(), uploads the photos and only then commits. In PostgreSQL now() is the transaction's start time, so all 86 rows of batch 5521 carry 01:52:07.413, although they became visible only at 02:19:51. At 02:05 the extract's snapshot could not see uncommitted rows (pid 4471 was idle in transaction since 01:52:07). It loaded 37 other rows and moved the watermark to 02:04:51. Every later run asks for updated_at greater than that, so those rows are skipped forever. Oct 20 and 23 show the same pattern, all from tablet-sync. The checkpoint warning is WAL tuning, and the source rows are intact. Recovery: re-extract Oct 19-24 by work_date and MERGE by record_id. That is idempotent, so rows already loaded are no-ops. Then rerun dbt for those dates and confirm the reconciliation reads zero missing before invoicing. Fix: replace the watermark query with log-based CDC (Debezium on a logical replication slot), which delivers changes in commit order. Until then, re-read a two-hour lookback and set idle_in_transaction_session_timeout to 10 minutes so no transaction outlives the margin. The app team should upload photos before opening the transaction. clock_timestamp() would not help, because visibility follows commit time. Prevention: a daily dbt test comparing source and lakehouse rows and footage per work date, and an alert on transactions open longer than five minutes.",
    alternatives:["Safe watermark: cap the watermark at the xact_start of the oldest open transaction (from pg_stat_activity, or the xmin of txid_current_snapshot()), and let the MERGE absorb re-reads. This is correct without new infrastructure, but it needs the extract role to see other sessions and still assumes every writer uses now().","Nightly full reload of the last 7 days with partition overwrite. This is simple and correct at this volume, but it does not scale and delays corrections by up to a day.","Switch updated_at to clock_timestamp() or set it in a trigger. This narrows the window but does not close it, because visibility follows commit time. It is partly right at best."]},
  hints:["Look at the updated_at values of the missing rows and at what the sync service was doing between BEGIN and COMMIT.","What time does PostgreSQL's now() return inside a transaction, and can another session see rows that are not committed yet?","The batch committed 27 minutes after its timestamp, after the watermark had already passed it. Re-extract by work date with the idempotent MERGE, then move to commit-ordered CDC or a lookback longer than any transaction can last."] }
,
{ id:"fcp37", kind:"case", title:"Two leaders, one invoice run", level:5, domains:["p37"],
  context:"You run the monthly billing cycle for a regional ISP's 4,812 business accounts. Two instances of billing-worker, a Java service, run on separate VMs. They elect a leader through etcd (a lease with a 15-second TTL, renewed every 5 seconds), and only the leader runs the cycle. The cycle processes accounts in batches of 50. For each account it calls the payment provider's charge API and records the charge, and after each batch it writes a checkpoint so a new leader can resume. Last night's October cycle completed and every dashboard is green, but this morning 50 customers in one narrow account range report being charged twice. Refunds are being prepared. Finance wants to know how two workers could both charge the same accounts, and wants a design in which that cannot happen.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"billing-a log (UTC)", text:"2026-10-26T02:00:00.114Z INFO  election: elected leader key=/billing/leader/694d8a3c2f1e07b3 create_rev=88123 ttl=15s\n2026-10-26T02:00:00.120Z INFO  cycle 2026-10 start: 4,812 accounts, 97 batches\n...\n2026-10-26T02:14:26.981Z DEBUG election: lease keepalive ok ttl=15s\n2026-10-26T02:14:30.188Z INFO  checkpoint written: last_batch=36\n2026-10-26T02:14:30.205Z INFO  batch 37/97 start (accounts 10351-10400)\n2026-10-26T02:14:31.893Z INFO  charge ok acct=10362 (12/50)\n2026-10-26T02:14:58.761Z WARN  [gc] Pause Full (G1 Compaction Pause) 3021M->2894M(3072M) 26.804s\n2026-10-26T02:14:58.905Z INFO  charge ok acct=10363 (13/50)\n2026-10-26T02:14:59.034Z WARN  election: keepalive failed: etcdserver: requested lease not found\n2026-10-26T02:14:59.035Z WARN  election: leadership lost (isLeader flag cleared)\n2026-10-26T02:15:04.132Z INFO  charge ok acct=10400 (50/50)\n2026-10-26T02:15:04.140Z ERROR not leader at batch boundary; stopping cycle, checkpoint NOT written"},
    {label:"billing-b log (UTC)", text:"2026-10-26T02:00:00.131Z INFO  election: campaigning key=/billing/leader/694d8a3c2f1e07c9 create_rev=88125 (waiting)\n2026-10-26T02:14:42.016Z INFO  election: watch saw DELETE /billing/leader/694d8a3c2f1e07b3\n2026-10-26T02:14:42.031Z INFO  election: elected leader key=/billing/leader/694d8a3c2f1e07c9 create_rev=88125\n2026-10-26T02:14:42.090Z INFO  cycle 2026-10 resume from checkpoint last_batch=36\n2026-10-26T02:14:42.101Z INFO  batch 37/97 start (accounts 10351-10400)\n2026-10-26T02:14:49.170Z INFO  charge ok acct=10400 (50/50)\n2026-10-26T02:14:49.181Z INFO  checkpoint written: last_batch=37\n2026-10-26T02:14:49.190Z INFO  batch 38/97 start (accounts 10401-10450)\n...\n2026-10-26T02:25:30.552Z INFO  cycle 2026-10 complete: 4,812 accounts"},
    {label:"BillingCycle.java (abridged)", text:"for (int b = ckpt.lastBatch(cycle) + 1; b <= batches; b++) {\n    if (!election.isLeader()) {            // volatile flag, cleared by the keepalive thread\n        log.error(\"not leader at batch boundary; stopping cycle, checkpoint NOT written\");\n        return;\n    }\n    for (Account a : accountsInBatch(b)) {\n        Charge c = payments.charge(a.id(), a.amountCents());   // POST /v1/charges, no Idempotency-Key header\n        jdbc.update(\"UPDATE invoices SET status='paid', charge_id=? WHERE account_id=? AND cycle=?\",\n                    c.id(), a.id(), cycle);\n    }\n    jdbc.update(\"UPDATE billing_checkpoint SET last_batch=? WHERE cycle=?\", b, cycle);\n}"},
    {label:"Reconciliation, etcd server log and clocks", text:"payment provider export, accounts 10351-10400, cycle 2026-10:\n  100 charges: one set 02:14:30-02:15:04 (billing-a), one set 02:14:42-02:14:49 (billing-b)\ninvoices table, same accounts: 50 rows status='paid', one charge_id each\nall other accounts: exactly 1 provider charge\n\netcd-1 server log, same night:\n{\"level\":\"warn\",\"ts\":\"2026-10-26T02:13:58.402Z\",\"msg\":\"apply request took too long\",\"took\":\"132.6ms\",\"expected-duration\":\"100ms\"}\n{\"level\":\"warn\",\"ts\":\"2026-10-26T02:14:07.911Z\",\"msg\":\"slow fdatasync\",\"took\":\"1.21s\",\"expected-duration\":\"1s\"}\n\n$ chronyc tracking | grep 'System time'      (both VMs, 09:00)\nbilling-a  System time     : 0.000412 seconds fast of NTP time\nbilling-b  System time     : 0.000187 seconds slow of NTP time"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp37q1", q:"How could both workers charge accounts 10351-10400?", options:["etcd split-brain: its Raft cluster elected two leaders, so both workers held the lock at once","Clock skew between the VMs made billing-a think its lease still had time left","billing-a's 26.8 s GC pause outlasted the 15 s lease. etcd expired it at about 02:14:42 and billing-b was legitimately elected and resumed from checkpoint 36. billing-a woke at 02:14:58 mid-batch, learned of the lost lease a moment later, but checks only at batch boundaries, and nothing at the payment API or the database rejected a deposed leader's writes","billing-b ignored the lock because its watch fired early"], answer:2, why:"The two terms of leadership never overlapped inside etcd: A's key was deleted before B was elected, and B's create_rev is higher. The overlap existed only in A's head. Lease expiry is decided by the etcd leader's timer, so the client's wall clock does not matter, and chrony shows sub-millisecond offsets anyway."},
    {id:"fcp37q2", q:"A teammate proposes checking isLeader() before every single charge and raising the TTL to 60 s. Why does that not make double charging impossible?", options:["Both shrink the window but cannot close it. The process can pause between the check and the charge, and any finite TTL can be outlasted by a long enough pause or VM stall. A 60 s TTL also slows every genuine failover. Safety has to be enforced by the resource being written, not by the writer's belief that it is the leader","It does make it impossible, because no realistic GC pause lasts 60 s","It fails only because etcd leases are unreliable; Redis Redlock with five nodes would make the check safe","Checking before every charge is enough; the TTL change is only for performance"], answer:0, why:"This is the check-then-act gap Kleppmann described for Redlock. Any lock service, however well replicated, can tell a client only what was true when it last heard from it. The fix has to live at the resource: a fencing token checked by the database, plus idempotency at the payment API."},
    {id:"fcp37q3", q:"Which design makes a deposed leader's writes harmless?", options:["Run a single billing-worker instance so no election is needed","Make each worker sleep 15 s after resuming from any pause before it charges","Log the leader's hostname with every charge so finance can find duplicates afterwards","Use the leader key's etcd create revision as a fencing token: checkpoint and charge-record writes succeed only if the stored fence is not higher (zero rows updated means stop), every charge carries an idempotency key such as cycle:account_id so the provider returns the original charge instead of making a new one, and a unique constraint on (account_id, cycle) backs it up"], answer:3, why:"Successive leaders' keys have increasing create revisions (88123, then 88125), so a stale leader's token is always lower and the database can refuse it. The payment provider cannot check your fence, which is why the idempotency key is needed too. A single instance gives up availability and still double-charges on a crash and retry. A process cannot tell that it was paused. Logging only detects duplicates after the fact."},
    {id:"fcp37q4", q:"The invoices table shows each of the 50 accounts paid exactly once. Why, and what follows?", options:["The duplicates must be on the provider's side; the billing system itself is correct","The UPDATE overwrites charge_id unconditionally, so the second charge silently replaced the first: the table records the last write, not every charge. Reconcile and refund from the provider's ledger, and record charges as inserts with a unique key rather than an overwrite","The checkpoint stopped the duplicate writes reaching the invoices table","etcd's revision history deduplicated the writes"], answer:1, why:"Last-write-wins storage hides concurrent writers, which is why a green dashboard coexisted with 50 double charges. The provider export is the source of truth for money that moved. Inserting into a charges table under a unique constraint turns a second charge attempt into a visible error instead of a silent overwrite."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the incident review for finance and engineering: the exact sequence of events with timestamps, why the existing leader election could not prevent it, the remediation for the affected customers, and a redesign that makes duplicate charges impossible even when two workers both believe they lead, with how you will test it.",
    rubric:["Reconstructs the timeline: last keepalive 02:14:26.98; GC pause from about 02:14:31.96 to 02:14:58.76 (26.8 s, longer than the 15 s TTL); lease expiry around 02:14:42; B elected at 02:14:42.03 and resumed at batch 37; A charged 38 more accounts until 02:15:04 despite losing leadership at 02:14:59.","Explains why a lease or lock alone cannot guarantee mutual exclusion (process pauses, the check-then-act gap), and why per-charge checks or a longer TTL only shrink the window.","Rules out etcd split-brain and clock skew with evidence: sequential elections with increasing create revisions, chrony offsets under 1 ms, sub-second etcd warnings logged before the incident, and server-side lease expiry.","Designs fencing: the leader key's create revision (88123 versus 88125) as a token checked by conditional writes on the checkpoint and charge records, plus provider idempotency keys (cycle:account_id) and a unique constraint on charges.","Remediates from the provider's ledger, not the invoices table: refunds the 50 second charges, corrects the overwritten charge_id values and notifies the customers.","Tests the design with fault injection: pause the leader (SIGSTOP, or a forced long GC) for longer than the TTL during a staging cycle and assert exactly one provider charge per account. Treats GC tuning as a secondary measure, not the fix."],
    reference:"At 02:14:26.98 billing-a renewed its 15-second lease for the last time. At 02:14:31.96, twelve charges into batch 37, its JVM entered a 26.8-second full GC pause. etcd expired the lease at about 02:14:42 and deleted A's key. billing-b, next in line, was elected at 02:14:42.03, resumed from checkpoint 36 and charged all 50 accounts of batch 37 by 02:14:49. A woke at 02:14:58.76 mid-batch and charged the remaining 38 accounts until 02:15:04. Its keepalive thread learned of the lost lease at 02:14:59, but the loop checks leadership only between batches, and neither the payment API nor the database could reject a deposed leader. Result: 50 accounts charged twice, while the invoices table shows one charge each because the UPDATE overwrote charge_id. etcd behaved correctly: the elections were sequential, clocks agree within a millisecond, and its warnings were sub-second and earlier. Checking before each charge or raising the TTL only shrinks the window, because a pause can land between the check and the act. Fix: use the leader key's create revision (88123 for A, 88125 for B) as a fencing token. Checkpoint and charge-record writes are conditional on the token, and zero rows updated means stop. Every charge sends Idempotency-Key cycle:account_id, so a repeat returns the original charge, backed by a unique (account_id, cycle) constraint. Refund from the provider's ledger. Test by stopping the leader with SIGSTOP for 30 s in staging and asserting one charge per account.",
    alternatives:["Drop the lock entirely: partition accounts across workers through a queue with one consumer per partition (single-writer ownership) and idempotent processing. This removes the election, but redelivery still requires idempotency keys at the provider.","Claim each invoice in the database first, by inserting a charge-intent row under a unique constraint or using SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, and only then call the provider. This works because the database becomes the arbiter, but the HTTP charge cannot join the transaction, so it still needs an idempotency key.","Tune the JVM to avoid 26 s pauses (right-size the heap, stop swapping, use a low-pause collector). Worth doing, but it lowers the probability rather than providing safety. Partly right at best."]},
  hints:["Line up billing-a's GC pause with the lease TTL and billing-b's election time. Who was the leader at 02:14:45?","A lease tells a process it was leader the last time it heard from etcd. What stops a process that does not yet know it lost leadership from writing?","A 26.8 s pause beat the 15 s TTL, so both workers charged batch 37. Fence every write with the leader key's create revision, send idempotency keys to the provider, and refund from the provider's ledger."] },
{ id:"fcp38", kind:"case", title:"Eight threads slower than one", level:3, domains:["p38","p15"],
  context:"Your business wrote logscan, a C tool that scans a day of syslog and OLT alarm archives (24 GB, already in the page cache of the analysis server) and counts lines and matches per worker thread, summing the counts after the threads join. A client wants the daily report in under 5 seconds so they can run it on demand. The server has an 8-core Intel Xeon E-2388G (64-byte cache lines) and dual-channel DDR4-3200, about 51.2 GB/s peak. One thread takes 18.4 s, and you expected 8 threads to finish in under 3 s. Instead, 8 threads are slower than 1. One coworker says the box is overheating and wants to open a hardware ticket. Another says the scan is memory-bandwidth bound and needs a bigger server. You have the timings, perf output and the source.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Thread sweep, plus the thermal check", text:"$ gcc -O2 -pthread logscan.c match.c report.c -o logscan\n$ for t in 1 2 4 8; do /usr/bin/time -f \"%e s  %P CPU\" ./logscan -t $t /data/archive/2026-10-25/; done\nthreads=1  lines=212448019  matches=1906233   18.41 s  99% CPU\nthreads=2  lines=212448019  matches=1906233   12.87 s  198% CPU\nthreads=4  lines=212448019  matches=1906233   14.12 s  396% CPU\nthreads=8  lines=212448019  matches=1906233   19.63 s  795% CPU\n\n$ dmesg -T | grep -i -E 'thermal|throttl'\n[Tue Oct 20 14:02:51 2026] mce: CPU3: Core temperature above threshold, cpu clock throttled (total events = 4)\n[Tue Oct 20 14:02:51 2026] mce: CPU3: Core temperature/speed normal\n\n$ sudo turbostat --quiet --show Busy%,Bzy_MHz,CoreTmp --interval 5     (during the 8-thread run)\nBusy%   Bzy_MHz  CoreTmp\n99.41   4012     71"},
    {label:"perf stat, 1 thread vs 8 threads", text:"$ perf stat -e cycles,instructions ./logscan -t 1 /data/archive/2026-10-25/\n    82,861,409,117      cycles\n   191,410,274,880      instructions        #    2.31  insn per cycle\n      18.43 seconds time elapsed\n\n$ perf stat -e cycles,instructions ./logscan -t 8 /data/archive/2026-10-25/\n   628,557,902,413      cycles\n   203,118,660,251      instructions        #    0.32  insn per cycle\n      19.66 seconds time elapsed"},
    {label:"perf c2c report (8 threads, excerpt) and symbol addresses", text:"$ perf c2c record -- ./logscan -t 8 /data/archive/2026-10-25/ && perf c2c report --stdio\n  Shared Data Cache Line Table\n  Index   Cacheline          Tot Hitm   LclHitm   Stores     Symbol\n      0   0x5612a8e4c0c0     96.81%     211,904   3,402,117  [.] worker\n      1   0x5612a8e4c100      2.77%       6,071     411,320  [.] worker\n  Shared Cache Line Distribution (cacheline 0x5612a8e4c0c0)\n  LclHitm   Off    cpu cnt   Symbol   Source:Line\n   12.9%    0x0    1         worker   logscan.c:48\n   12.6%    0x8    1         worker   logscan.c:48\n   12.4%    0x10   1         worker   logscan.c:48\n   ...      0x18-0x38: one CPU each, all logscan.c:48\n\n$ nm -n logscan | grep -E ' (lines|matches)$'\n00000000000040c0 B lines\n0000000000004100 B matches\n(PIE load base in this run: 0x5612a8e48000)"},
    {label:"logscan.c (excerpt)", text:"#define MAX_THREADS 8\nuint64_t lines[MAX_THREADS];      /* per-thread counters, summed by report.c after join */\nuint64_t matches[MAX_THREADS];\n\nint match_line(const char *b, const char *e);   /* defined in match.c */\n\nstatic void *worker(void *arg) {\n    int id = *(int *)arg;\n    const char *p = chunk_begin(id), *end = chunk_end(id);\n    while (p < end) {\n        const char *eol = memchr(p, '\\n', end - p);\n        if (!eol) eol = end - 1;\n        lines[id]++;                                   /* logscan.c:48 */\n        if (match_line(p, eol)) matches[id]++;         /* logscan.c:49 */\n        p = eol + 1;\n    }\n    return NULL;\n}"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp38q1", q:"Is the 8-thread run limited by memory bandwidth?", options:["Yes: eight threads reading 24 GB saturate DRAM","No: 24 GB in 19.6 s is about 1.2 GB/s, a few percent of the 51.2 GB/s peak, and IPC collapsed from 2.31 to 0.32 while every core reports busy at 4 GHz. The cores are stalling on something other than bandwidth","Yes, because data in the page cache is slower than ordinary DRAM","It cannot be known without benchmarking a bigger server"], answer:1, why:"The page cache is ordinary DRAM. A bandwidth-bound program would sit near the memory system's limit and stop scaling there, not get slower than one thread while using a twentieth of the bandwidth. Eight cores burn 7.6 times the cycles for 6% more instructions, which is the signature of stalls."},
    {id:"fcp38q2", q:"What does the perf c2c report show?", options:["A data race: the threads write the same counter, so the totals are wrong","The page cache is being evicted by the scan","Instruction-cache misses inside worker()","False sharing: lines[0..7] are eight 8-byte counters in one 64-byte cache line (offsets 0x0-0x38, one CPU each, all at logscan.c:48). Every increment needs that line in the Modified state, so the coherence protocol moves it between cores on almost every input line, and the HITMs are loads that found it modified in another core's cache"], answer:3, why:"lines starts at 0x40c0 (0x5612a8e48000 + 0x40c0 = 0x5612a8e4c0c0) and is exactly 64 bytes, so the entire array is one line. Each thread touches only its own element, so the totals are correct and identical across runs; the cost is coherence traffic, not wrong answers. matches is the warm second line because matches are rarer."},
    {id:"fcp38q3", q:"Why doesn't the compiler keep lines[id] in a register and avoid the memory traffic?", options:["lines is a global array other files can see, and match_line() lives in another translation unit, so the compiler must assume the call might read or change lines[id]. It stores the counter before the call and reloads it after, on every input line","-O2 never keeps array elements in registers; -O3 would","uint64_t does not fit in an x86-64 register","pthreads code is compiled without optimization"], answer:0, why:"Without seeing match_line's body, the optimizer has to keep memory consistent across the call. That turns a register increment into a load and a store to the shared line for every one of the 212 million lines. LTO or a local accumulator removes the problem; -O3 alone does not."},
    {id:"fcp38q4", q:"Which fix gives near-linear scaling with the least risk?", options:["Make the counters _Atomic and use atomic_fetch_add","Protect the counters with a mutex","Count into local variables inside worker() and write lines[id] and matches[id] once at the end, or give each thread its own cache-line-aligned struct. Then verify: perf c2c no longer shows the hot line, IPC returns above 2, and 8 threads finish near 18.4 / 8, about 2.3 to 2.6 s","Pin each thread to a core with taskset so the cache line stays put"], answer:2, why:"Atomics and mutexes add locked read-modify-write operations on the same line, so they make the ping-pong worse. Pinning keeps threads on fixed cores, but the line still has to move between those cores. Local accumulation removes the shared writes entirely and needs no new data structures."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the engineering note for the client and your coworkers: the diagnosis with the measurements that prove it, why the overheating and bandwidth theories are wrong, the code fix, the before-and-after measurements you will show, and a rule for future multithreaded code.",
    rubric:["Diagnoses false sharing on lines[], and to a lesser degree matches[]: eight per-thread 8-byte counters in one 64-byte line, shown by the perf c2c offsets 0x0-0x38 and the nm address 0x40c0.","Explains the mechanism: each increment needs exclusive ownership of the line (MESI Modified), so the line migrates between cores and IPC falls from 2.31 to 0.32. Notes that the external call to match_line forces a store and reload for every input line.","Refutes the bandwidth theory with arithmetic (about 1.2 GB/s against 51.2 GB/s) and the thermal theory with evidence (a single dmesg event from Oct 20; turbostat shows 4.0 GHz at 71 C during the run).","Fixes with thread-local accumulation written once per thread, or per-thread counters padded or aligned to a cache line. Rejects atomics, mutexes and pinning with reasons.","Verifies before and after: the thread-sweep table (8 threads near 2.3 to 2.6 s, under the 5 s target), IPC above 2, no hot shared line in perf c2c, identical totals.","States a reusable rule: per-thread mutable data lives in locals or on its own cache line, and scaling claims are checked with perf c2c. Mentions 128-byte lines or adjacent-line prefetch on some CPUs."],
    reference:"logscan does not scale because its per-thread counters share one cache line. lines[8] is 64 bytes at 0x40c0, exactly one line, and perf c2c attributes 96.8% of HITMs to it, with offsets 0x0 to 0x38 each written by a different CPU at logscan.c:48. Each thread writes only its own element, so the totals are correct. But every increment needs the line in the Modified state in that core's L1, so the coherence protocol moves it from core to core on almost every one of 212 million input lines. Because lines is a global array and match_line() lives in another file, the compiler must store lines[id] before each call and reload it afterwards, so every input line touches the shared line. IPC falls from 2.31 to 0.32 while every core shows busy. Neither alternative theory fits. The scan moves about 1.2 GB/s against 51.2 GB/s of memory bandwidth, and the only thermal event was on Oct 20, while turbostat shows 4.0 GHz at 71 C during the run. Fix: count into local variables inside worker() and write lines[id] and matches[id] once at the end. Alternatively, give each thread an alignas(64) struct. Verify with the thread sweep (8 threads should land near 2.3 to 2.6 s, under the 5 s target), IPC above 2 in perf stat, no hot line in perf c2c and unchanged totals. Rule for the team: per-thread mutable state lives in locals or on its own cache line, and every scaling claim comes with a perf c2c run.",
    alternatives:["Align each thread's counters to 128 bytes instead of 64. This is harmless on x86 and necessary on CPUs with 128-byte lines (Apple M-series) or adjacent-line prefetching. Fine if the reason is documented.","Rewrite the loop with OpenMP and reduction(+:...) on per-thread counts. This produces the same thread-local accumulation automatically, and is acceptable if the team already uses OpenMP.","Make match_line static inline or build with -flto so the compiler can keep the counter in a register. This often works, but it depends on the optimizer, and the shared layout stays a trap for the next edit. Partly right."]},
  hints:["Work out the bandwidth the 8-thread run actually achieves, and compare IPC at 1 and 8 threads. Is this a throughput limit or a stall?","perf c2c shows one hot cache line written by eight CPUs at different offsets. What lives at 0x40c0, and how big is it?","lines[8] fills exactly one 64-byte line, so the coherence protocol moves it between cores on every input line. Accumulate in locals and write once per thread (or align each thread's counters to 64 bytes), then rerun the sweep and perf c2c."] }
,
{ id:"fcp39", kind:"case", title:"Photos that arrive after dark", level:4, domains:["p39"],
  context:"Your company maintains FieldSnap, the Android app a fiber contractor's 60 technicians use to photograph splice trays, closures and OTDR screens. QA reviews the photos the same afternoon so crews can fix problems before they leave a site. Version 3.8.0 reached 100% of devices on Oct 15, and its release notes say 'save company data: upload photos on Wi-Fi only'. Since then QA reports that most photos arrive between 7 p.m. and midnight, some technicians' photos not until the next morning, and a few phones have shown 'Pending 300+' for days. The operations manager also forwarded September's cellular bill, which is why that change was made. You need to restore same-day QA without blowing up the data plan.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Server-side upload metrics, Oct 12-25", text:"upload delay = server receive time - capture time\n\nversion  devices  photos/day  median delay  p90 delay    dup-UUID uploads/day\n3.7.2    60       5,880       4 min         38 min       14\n3.8.0    60       5,910       9 h 41 min    21 h 05 min  2,317\n\ndup-UUID upload: the server already had that photo, answered 200 {\"status\":\"duplicate\"} and discarded the body\ntop devices by dup-UUID uploads on Oct 24: tech-31 1,164   tech-07 812   tech-44 341\n\nSeptember cellular bill (3.7.2 era): pooled plan, 60 lines, 900 GB included\n  used 1,180 GB (FieldSnap photo uploads 676 GB), overage 280 GB x $10/GB = $2,800"},
    {label:"UploadScheduler.kt diff (3.7.2 to 3.8.0) and UploadWorker.kt", text:"  val constraints = Constraints.Builder()\n-     .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.CONNECTED)\n+     .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.UNMETERED)     // \"Wi-Fi only, save company data\"\n      .build()\n  val req = OneTimeWorkRequestBuilder<UploadWorker>()\n      .setConstraints(constraints)\n      .setBackoffCriteria(BackoffPolicy.EXPONENTIAL, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)\n      .build()\n  WorkManager.getInstance(ctx).enqueueUniqueWork(\"upload\", ExistingWorkPolicy.KEEP, req)\n\n// UploadWorker.kt, unchanged since 3.2\noverride suspend fun doWork(): Result {\n    val pending = dao.pendingPhotos()                  // the whole backlog, oldest first\n    val done = mutableListOf<Long>()\n    try { for (p in pending) { api.upload(p.uuid, p.file); done += p.id } }\n    catch (e: IOException) { return Result.retry() }\n    dao.markUploaded(done)                             // one commit, at the very end\n    return Result.success()\n}"},
    {label:"tech-31 phone (Pixel 8a, Android 16), FieldSnap 3.8.0 debug log, Oct 23-24", text:"07:12:40 UploadScheduler enqueue: pending=1 constraint=UNMETERED network=cellular(metered)\n11:58:02 UploadScheduler state=ENQUEUED pending=212 runAttemptCount=0 network=cellular(metered)\n16:31:15 UploadScheduler state=ENQUEUED pending=388 runAttemptCount=0 network=cellular(metered)\n21:42:10 UploadWorker start pending=388 (2.02 GB) network=wifi(home, unmetered)\n21:52:10 UploadWorker stopped: reason=TIMEOUT after 600 s, uploaded 141/388, markUploaded not reached\n21:52:41 UploadWorker start pending=388 (attempt 2)\n22:02:41 UploadWorker stopped: reason=TIMEOUT after 600 s, uploaded 139/388 (all 139 answered duplicate)\n22:03:42 UploadWorker start pending=388 (attempt 3)\n... 9 attempts overnight, each stopped at 600 s after about 140 photos\n07:05:12 network=cellular(metered); work waiting for UNMETERED, pending=391"},
    {label:"logcat on tech-31, Oct 24 09:14 (repeats every 15 min)", text:"W/System.err: javax.net.ssl.SSLHandshakeException: java.security.cert.CertPathValidatorException:\nW/System.err:     Trust anchor for certification path not found.\nW/System.err:     at com.android.org.conscrypt.SSLUtils.toSSLHandshakeException(SSLUtils.java:363)\nW/System.err:     at com.vendoranalytics.sdk.net.Uploader.flush(Uploader.kt:88)\nW/System.err:     ... 14 more\nnote: FieldSnap's own uploads go to files.fieldsnap.example; every api.upload call in the log above returned 200"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp39q1", q:"Why do most photos now arrive in the evening?", options:["Doze and App Standby defer WorkManager jobs while the phone rides in the truck","The server's ingest queue is backed up","3.8.0 requires an unmetered network, and technicians are on LTE all day, so the work's constraint is never met until the phone joins home Wi-Fi","The SSL handshake errors are blocking the uploads"], answer:2, why:"The log shows the work ENQUEUED with runAttemptCount 0 and the network listed as metered cellular: the work never ran, rather than ran and was deferred. Doze needs a stationary, screen-off device, and these phones are in use all day. Server-side delay tracks the app version, not server load."},
    {id:"fcp39q2", q:"Why do tech-31's photos 142-388 never arrive, while the server logs 1,164 duplicate uploads from that phone in one night?", options:["The server's 'duplicate' answer makes the worker fail and give up","The worker sends the whole backlog in one run but marks photos uploaded only at the end. At about 10 Mbps, 2.02 GB needs roughly 27 minutes, but a WorkManager worker is stopped after 10, so every retry starts again from photo 1, re-sends the ~140 the server already has, and is stopped before reaching anything new. The UUIDs prevent duplicate records, not wasted transfers","The home Wi-Fi is metered, so the constraint stops the work","The five-hour backoff cap allows only one attempt per night"], answer:1, why:"141 photos of about 5.2 MB in 600 s is about 1.2 MB/s, roughly 10 Mbps. Being stopped is not an IOException, so the catch never runs, markUploaded is never reached, and the next attempt reads the same 388 rows. Nine attempts times about 140 re-sent photos matches the 1,164 duplicates. Idempotency protected the server's data but not the phone's progress."},
    {id:"fcp39q3", q:"Which change restores same-day QA without a large cellular bill?", options:["Upload a QA-size rendition (for example 2,048 px, about 0.6 MB) over any connection as soon as each photo is taken, and send originals later with UNMETERED. Make each photo its own small unit of work, or mark each one uploaded the moment the server acknowledges it, and use resumable uploads for the large originals","Revert to CONNECTED and keep the worker as it is","Run a foreground service holding a partial wake lock all day that uploads continuously","Ask technicians to disable battery optimization for FieldSnap and keep a hotspot on"], answer:0, why:"QA needs a viewable photo, not the 5 MB original. Renditions cost about 5,910 x 0.6 MB x 22 days, roughly 78 GB a month, against 676 GB for originals. Per-item commit ends the restart loop on slow links. Reverting alone brings back the overage and keeps the loop. An all-day wake lock drains batteries and runs into Google Play's wake-lock enforcement."},
    {id:"fcp39q4", q:"How should you treat the SSLHandshakeException in logcat?", options:["It is why uploads fail on cellular; fix the certificate and they will flow","It shows that certificate pinning broke in 3.8.0","It means the phone's clock is wrong","As unrelated: the stack trace is inside a third-party analytics SDK calling its own endpoint (probably intercepted by the fleet's filtering VPN), while FieldSnap's own uploads return 200 whenever they run. The problem is when uploads run, not whether TLS works. File it separately"], answer:3, why:"Read the frames: the failure is in com.vendoranalytics.sdk, not in FieldSnap's networking. 'Trust anchor not found' means that SDK's server chain did not validate on this device, which a filtering proxy causes, or a misconfigured server. It is worth a ticket, because an SDK silently failing to send data is a privacy and quality question, but it does not explain photos waiting for Wi-Fi."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the fix proposal and release plan for the operations manager: why photos arrive late, why some never arrive, the code and policy changes (with the cellular data arithmetic), how you will roll out and verify the fix, and which tests would have caught this.",
    rubric:["Explains the UNMETERED constraint against all-day LTE use, citing the diff and the ENQUEUED, runAttemptCount=0 log lines on cellular, and rules out Doze.","Explains the stuck backlog: the all-or-nothing commit plus WorkManager's 10-minute execution limit on a roughly 10 Mbps uplink (2.02 GB is about 27 minutes), so each attempt re-sends the same ~140 photos. Ties this to the 2,317 duplicate-UUID uploads per day.","Proposes a design with per-photo (or small-batch) work and per-item commit, a QA-size rendition over any network with originals on Wi-Fi, and resumable uploads (or a long-running or user-initiated transfer) for large backlogs, keeping the UUID idempotency.","Does the data math: about 676 GB a month of originals over cellular versus about 78 GB a month at 0.6 MB per rendition, which keeps the pool under 900 GB.","Rolls out through a Play staged rollout (for example 10%, 50%, 100%) gated on a median delay under 15 minutes, p90 under 1 hour and duplicate uploads back near the 3.7.2 baseline, with a plan for phones that already hold large backlogs.","Prevents recurrence with tests for constraint behaviour and for resuming after a stopped worker without redoing finished items, plus a rule that constraint changes need a delay target agreed with QA. Treats the SDK's TLS error as a separate ticket."],
    reference:"Late photos come from one line in 3.8.0: the upload work now requires NetworkType.UNMETERED. Technicians are on LTE all day, so the job sits ENQUEUED with runAttemptCount 0 until the phone joins home Wi-Fi, which is why the median delay jumped from 4 minutes to 9 h 41 min. Missing photos come from an older flaw that the change exposed. UploadWorker sends the whole backlog in one run and marks rows uploaded only at the end. A WorkManager worker is stopped after 10 minutes. tech-31's 388 photos (2.02 GB) need about 27 minutes on a 10 Mbps home uplink, so every attempt re-sends the first ~140 photos (the server answers 'duplicate'), is stopped, and starts over. That is 1,164 wasted uploads in one night and nothing new delivered. The fix: enqueue one small unit of work per photo, and mark each photo done when the server acknowledges it. Upload a 2,048 px QA rendition (about 0.6 MB) over any network as soon as it is taken, and send originals with UNMETERED and resumable uploads. Data cost: originals over LTE were about 676 GB a month; renditions are about 78 GB, so the pool stays under its 900 GB. Ship through a staged rollout at 10%, 50%, then 100%, gated on a median delay under 15 minutes, p90 under 1 hour, and duplicate uploads back near 14 a day. Add tests for unmet constraints and for resuming after a stopped worker. The analytics SDK's TLS error is a separate ticket.",
    alternatives:["Revert to CONNECTED and fix the per-item commit. This restores same-day delivery and ends the retry loop, but puts about 676 GB a month of originals back on the cellular pool, so it is only acceptable if the overage cost is accepted.","When the technician taps 'Upload now', run the backlog as a user-initiated data transfer job (Android 14 and later) or a foreground worker via setForeground with the dataSync type. Good for large backlogs, but it must respect foreground-service time limits and show a visible notification.","Keep Wi-Fi only and add Wi-Fi at the equipment yard. Cheap, but QA still waits until crews return, so it misses the same-day requirement. Partly right."]},
  hints:["Compare what changed between 3.7.2 and 3.8.0 with the network type in tech-31's log while the work waits.","Look at where markUploaded is called and how long a WorkManager worker may run. How long does 2.02 GB take at about 10 Mbps?","UNMETERED keeps the work waiting all day. At home, one all-or-nothing worker is stopped at 10 minutes and restarts from photo 1. Commit per photo, send a small QA rendition on any network and originals on Wi-Fi, and roll out in stages."] },
{ id:"fcp40", kind:"case", title:"Rubber-banding when the lobby fills", level:4, domains:["p40"],
  context:"Your two-person studio's co-op shooter, built in Unity with a custom UDP netcode layer, has a public playtest this weekend. The dedicated server runs on one cloud VM and simulates at 60 Hz. Every tick it sends each client a full snapshot of every replicated entity: players plus their drones and projectiles, about 7 entities per player. Internal tests with 8 to 16 players felt perfect. Last night's open test filled lobbies to 32, and from about 24 players up everyone rubber-banded: enemies teleported, players snapped back after moving, and the community Discord filled with 'unplayable'. The server's tick time looks healthy. You have the telemetry and need a fix that ships before Saturday.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Playtest telemetry by lobby size", text:"snapshot_B = per-client snapshot payload: 40-byte header + 52 bytes per entity, sent 60 times a second\n\nplayers  entities  snapshot_B  tick_p95_ms  server_out_Mbps  client_loss%  corrections/min  RTT_ms\n8        56        2,952       3.1          11.3             0.0           2                38\n16       112       5,864       4.0          45.0             0.1           3                39\n20       140       7,320       4.4          70.3             0.2           5                41\n24       168       8,776       4.9          99.6             6.8           148              212\n32       224       11,688      5.6          100.0 (flat)     19.4          611              480"},
    {label:"Server VM: spec and NIC counters during a 32-player lobby", text:"VM size: 4 vCPU, network baseline 100 Mbps (bursts above baseline last only minutes)\n\n$ ethtool -S ens5 | grep allowance            # at 21:40\n     bw_in_allowance_exceeded: 0\n     bw_out_allowance_exceeded: 1882143\n     pps_allowance_exceeded: 0\n     conntrack_allowance_exceeded: 0\n     linklocal_allowance_exceeded: 0\n$ ethtool -S ens5 | grep bw_out                # at 21:50\n     bw_out_allowance_exceeded: 2917604"},
    {label:"Netcode configuration and the serialized entity", text:"# NetConfig.asset (server)\ntickRate: 60\nsnapshotRate: 60            # one full snapshot per tick\ndeltaCompression: false\nrelevancy: All              # every client receives every entity\nmaxDatagram: 0              # 0 = send each snapshot as one UDP datagram and let IP fragment it\n\n// EntityState as written to the wire\nstruct EntityState { uint id; Vector3 pos; Quaternion rot; Vector3 vel; uint anim; float health; }\n// 4 + 12 + 16 + 12 + 4 + 4 = 52 bytes, all full-precision"},
    {label:"Client net graph and server log, 32-player lobby", text:"client net graph (player 'ky_r', 21:46)\n  snapshot 11.7 KB = 8 IP fragments | fragment loss 2.6% | snapshot loss 19.4%\n  interp buffer 100 ms | buffer underruns 41/min | prediction corrections 611/min | RTT 480 ms\n\nserver log\n21:38:02 WARN  GC.Collect spike 41 ms (managed heap 1.9 GB)\n21:42:00 INFO  tick p95 5.6 ms, max 47.0 ms (budget 16.7 ms)\n21:47:55 WARN  GC.Collect spike 39 ms (managed heap 1.9 GB)"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp40q1", q:"Why does the server's outbound traffic grow so much faster than the player count?", options:["TCP retransmissions multiply as more players join","The server tick takes longer with more players, so it sends more data","Each extra player sends more input packets, which the server echoes to everyone","Each of N clients receives every entity, and entities grow with N (about 7 per player), so bytes per tick grow with N x 7N, roughly quadratically. 60 snapshots/s x 11,688 B x 32 clients is about 180 Mbps of demand, against a VM that sustains about 100 Mbps"], answer:3, why:"Doubling players from 16 to 32 multiplied demand by about four (45 to 180 Mbps). The measured output flattens at the 100 Mbps baseline, and bw_out_allowance_exceeded climbs by about a million packets in ten minutes: the provider is shaping and dropping the excess. The protocol is UDP, so TCP plays no part."},
    {id:"fcp40q2", q:"Fragment loss is only 2.6%, yet 19.4% of snapshots are lost. Why?", options:["Each 11.7 KB snapshot is one UDP datagram split into 8 IP fragments, and losing any one fragment loses the whole datagram: 1 - 0.974^8 is about 19%. Oversized datagrams amplify loss, which is why netcode keeps each packet under the path MTU","The client discards snapshots older than its interpolation buffer","UDP checksums fail more often on large packets","Routers reorder fragments, and the client drops reordered packets"], answer:0, why:"IP reassembles a datagram only when every fragment arrives; there is no partial delivery. With 1,500-byte MTUs, 11,696 bytes of UDP payload need 8 fragments. At 16 players a snapshot needed 4, and loss was near zero anyway, which is why internal tests never showed it."},
    {id:"fcp40q3", q:"Which plan fixes the problem, not just this weekend's symptom?", options:["Send snapshots over TCP so nothing is lost","Raise the tick and snapshot rate to 128 Hz so corrections are smoother","Cut the bytes: send snapshots at 20 to 30 Hz with client interpolation, add interest management so each client gets only relevant nearby entities, quantize position and rotation (for example 16-bit fixed-point position, smallest-three quaternion), delta-compress against the last acknowledged snapshot, and cap datagrams at about 1,200 bytes. Treat a bigger VM as headroom, not the fix","Move to a VM with a 1 Gbps baseline and change nothing else"], answer:2, why:"TCP's head-of-line blocking stalls fresh positions behind lost stale ones, and 128 Hz doubles the traffic that is already too much. A bigger VM relieves the cap this weekend but leaves quadratic growth, 5.6 Mbps per client and 8-fragment snapshots that turn any loss on a player's own connection into rubber-banding."},
    {id:"fcp40q4", q:"How should you treat the GC.Collect spikes in the server log?", options:["As the cause: each spike stalls the server and every client rubber-bands","As a minor, separate issue: two spikes of about 40 ms ten minutes apart cannot produce 611 corrections a minute, tick p95 is 5.6 ms of a 16.7 ms budget, and the symptoms track player count and bandwidth, not time. Note it for later allocation and pooling work","As proof that the server needs more vCPUs","As proof the server is CPU-bound, so dropping the tick rate to 30 Hz is the fix"], answer:1, why:"A 40 ms pause drops two or three ticks once every ten minutes, a hiccup players barely notice. The rubber-banding is continuous above 24 players and absent below 20, with CPU headroom to spare at every size. Matching a symptom's rate and its dependence on load to a cause is how you separate signal from noise in a profiler."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the fix plan for the team: the diagnosis with the numbers, a bandwidth budget for 32 players after your changes, what ships before Saturday versus later, and how you will verify it with a load test.",
    rubric:["Diagnoses bandwidth saturation: demand grows roughly with N squared (N clients x about 7N entities x 52 B x 60 Hz), reaching about 100 Mbps at 24 players and 180 Mbps at 32 against a 100 Mbps baseline. Cites the flat 100 Mbps output and the climbing bw_out_allowance_exceeded counter.","Explains loss amplification from IP fragmentation (8 fragments turn 2.6% loss into about 19%), and how loss plus queueing (RTT from 38 to 480 ms) empties the interpolation buffer and forces prediction corrections, which players see as rubber-banding.","Rules out CPU and GC with the evidence: tick p95 of 5.6 ms against 16.7 ms, and GC spikes far too rare for the symptom.","Proposes byte reductions with a computed budget, for example 30 Hz, about 18-byte quantized entities, a relevancy cap near 40 entities and about 50% from delta compression, giving about 0.1 Mbps per client and about 3 Mbps for 32 players, with datagrams of 1,200 bytes or less.","Separates the Saturday plan (snapshot rate, relevancy, MTU cap, and a temporary larger VM or a 20-player cap) from later work such as full delta compression and priority accumulation.","Verifies with a bot load test at 32 to 40 players under netem loss and latency, gated on output well under the baseline, snapshot loss under 1%, corrections near the 8-player level and flat allowance counters."],
    reference:"The server is not slow; its network link is full. Each tick, every one of N clients gets a full snapshot of every entity, and entities grow at about 7 per player, so traffic grows with N x 7N. At 52 bytes per entity and 60 snapshots a second, 24 players need about 101 Mbps and 32 need about 180 Mbps, while the VM sustains about 100 Mbps. The telemetry flattens at exactly 100 Mbps, and bw_out_allowance_exceeded climbs by a million packets in ten minutes: the provider drops the excess. Fragmentation multiplies the damage. An 11.7 KB snapshot is one datagram in 8 IP fragments, so a 2.6% fragment loss becomes 19% snapshot loss, and queueing pushes RTT from 38 to 480 ms. The 100 ms interpolation buffer runs dry and prediction keeps correcting: rubber-banding. Tick p95 is 5.6 ms of 16.7, and two 40 ms GC spikes ten minutes apart cannot cause 611 corrections a minute. For Saturday: drop snapshots to 30 Hz with interpolation, send only entities within relevance range (cap about 40), quantize position and rotation to about 18 bytes, cap datagrams at 1,200 bytes, and cap lobbies at 20 until the load test passes. Next: delta compression against the last acknowledged snapshot. Budget: 30 x (40 + 40 x 18 x 0.5) is about 12 KB/s, roughly 0.1 Mbps per client and 3 Mbps for 32 players. Verify with 40 bots under netem loss: snapshot loss under 1%, corrections near the 8-player baseline, allowance counters flat.",
    alternatives:["Rent a VM with a 1 Gbps sustained baseline for the weekend. This buys immediate relief at 32 players, but traffic still grows quadratically, each client still needs about 5.6 Mbps, and fragmentation loss remains on weak connections. A stopgap, not the fix.","Adopt an established netcode library's snapshot interpolation, delta compression and interest management instead of hand-rolling them. Less custom code to maintain, but a larger change to land before Saturday.","Lower the tick rate to 30 Hz along with the snapshot rate. This halves bandwidth, but it also makes input feel less responsive. Decoupling the snapshot rate from the tick rate gets the bandwidth saving without that cost. Partly right."]},
  hints:["Plot server_out_Mbps against players. What happens at 24, and what do the VM's spec and the allowance counter say?","How many IP fragments does an 11.7 KB datagram need, and what happens to the datagram if one fragment is lost?","Bytes grow with players x entities, the VM caps at 100 Mbps, and 8-fragment snapshots turn 2.6% loss into 19%. Send fewer, smaller, relevant snapshots: 30 Hz, interest management, quantization, delta compression, datagrams under 1,200 bytes."] }
,
{ id:"fcp41", kind:"case", title:"The right answer, read backwards", level:5, domains:["p41","p14"],
  context:"A community college asked your business to run a live 'quantum search' demo for its IT students next Thursday on real IBM hardware, using the Open Plan (10 QPU minutes per rolling 28 days). A junior teammate built a 3-qubit Grover search in Qiskit for the target he writes as 110 (q0 = 1, q1 = 1, q2 = 0): two iterations, 4,000 shots. He reports two problems. First, the ideal simulator 'returns the wrong answer', 011. Second, on ibm_kingston the answer he expected, 110, appears only 8.7% of the time, which he calls 'the hardware being broken'. His plan is to 'fix' the oracle so it marks 011 and rerun with 100,000 shots. Review his work before he spends the QPU budget.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"grover_demo.py (excerpt)", text:"def oracle(qc):                    # mark |110>, written q0 q1 q2\n    qc.x(2)\n    qc.h(2); qc.ccx(0, 1, 2); qc.h(2) # CCZ\n    qc.x(2)\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3)\nqc.h([0, 1, 2])\nfor _ in range(2):                 # k = 2 iterations\n    oracle(qc); diffusion(qc)       # diffusion = H X CCZ X H on all three\nqc.measure_all()\n\nlayout = [0, 47, 121]               # \"the three best T1 values on the chip\"\npm = generate_preset_pass_manager(optimization_level=1, backend=backend, initial_layout=layout)\nisa = pm.run(qc)\njob = Sampler(mode=backend).run([isa], shots=4000)"},
    {label:"Counts: ideal simulator vs ibm_kingston (4,000 shots each)", text:"AerSimulator (ideal):\n{'011': 3781, '000': 33, '001': 30, '010': 31, '100': 32, '101': 29, '110': 32, '111': 32}\n\nibm_kingston, config A (layout [0, 47, 121], level 1):\n{'011': 1083, '111': 512, '001': 498, '010': 471, '000': 402, '101': 371, '110': 349, '100': 314}\nQPU time used: 5 s\n\nteammate's note: \"Sim says 011, target is 110: oracle bug? Hardware gives 110 only 349/4000 = 8.7%.\""},
    {label:"Transpiled circuits and calibration excerpt (ibm_kingston, today)", text:"config  settings                                 physical qubits     swaps  cz   depth  duration\nA       level 1, initial_layout=[0, 47, 121]     0, 47, 121 (+path)  37     141  488    ~41 us\nB       level 3, no initial_layout, seed 7       62, 63, 64          4      32   112    ~9 us\n\nqubit  T1_us  T2_us  readout_err\n0      341    88     0.021\n47     356    102    0.018\n121    349    71     0.024\n62     238    190    0.011\n63     251    176    0.009\n64     229    201    0.012\n113    12     9      0.312   (flagged BAD on the dashboard)\nCZ error: chip median 2.0e-3; mean on config A's routed path 2.6e-3; (62,63) 1.6e-3, (63,64) 1.8e-3"},
    {label:"Error budget from calibration (F = CZ fidelity x idle decoherence x readout)", text:"predicted success = F x p_ideal + (1 - F) / 8         p_ideal(k) = sin^2((2k + 1) x theta), theta = arcsin(1/sqrt(8)) = 20.7 deg\n\nconfig  k  cz   duration  F      p_ideal  predicted  measured\nA       2  141  41 us     0.16   0.945    0.25       0.27\nA       1  70   20 us     0.39   0.781    0.38       -\nB       2  32   9 us      0.80   0.945    ?          -\nB       1  16   4.5 us    0.88   0.781    ?          -\n\nstandard error of a measured probability: sqrt(p (1 - p) / shots)\nteammate's plan: 100,000 shots of config A (about 125 s of QPU time)"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp41q1", q:"The ideal simulator returns '011' about 94.5% of the time. What does that mean?", options:["The oracle marks the wrong state and has to be changed to mark 011","measure_all() reverses the classical register on simulators only","The simulator's default noise model flipped two bits","Nothing is wrong: Qiskit prints bitstrings with qubit 0 as the rightmost character, so '011' means q2 = 0, q1 = 1, q0 = 1, exactly the intended target. 94.5% matches sin^2(5 theta) for two iterations with N = 8"], answer:3, why:"Qiskit's little-endian convention puts qubit 0 in the rightmost position of every string. The oracle flips q2 so that the CCZ fires on q0 = 1, q1 = 1, q2 = 0, which prints as '011'. AerSimulator is noiseless unless you attach a noise model, and the 3,781/4,000 peak is the textbook value."},
    {id:"fcp41q2", q:"What would the teammate's 'fix' (making the oracle mark 011 in his q0 q1 q2 notation) actually do?", options:["It would mark q0 = 0, q1 = 1, q2 = 1, a different item. The simulator would print '110' and look fixed, while the demo searched for the wrong answer. Compare against Qiskit's ordering instead, or reverse the strings only when displaying them","Nothing would change, because Grover's algorithm is symmetric in bit order","The circuit would fail to transpile","It would also fix the hardware result, because the new oracle uses fewer gates"], answer:0, why:"A test that passes because two misreadings cancel is the most dangerous kind. Bit order is a convention you must apply consistently from the problem statement to the display. The oracle gate count would not change, and neither would the noise."},
    {id:"fcp41q3", q:"Measured on hardware, the correct string '011' came back 27% of the time. What dominates that low figure?", options:["Too few shots: 4,000 shots cannot resolve a 94% peak","The pinned layout: three qubits chosen for best T1 but scattered across the chip, forcing 37 SWAPs (141 CZ, depth 488, about 41 us) on qubits whose T2 is only 71-102 us. The calibration model predicts 0.25 against a measured 0.27, so the hardware is behaving as its numbers say","Qubit 113's 31% readout error corrupts the results","Grover's algorithm cannot work on NISQ hardware"], answer:1, why:"Interference depends on phase coherence (T2), and choosing qubits by T1 alone ignored it. Connectivity matters more still: routing multiplied the two-qubit gate count by more than four. Qubit 113 is in neither layout. The standard error at 4,000 shots is about 0.7%, tiny next to a 70-point gap from the ideal."},
    {id:"fcp41q4", q:"Fill in the missing predictions in the error budget. Which configuration should the demo run?", options:["Config A with one iteration, because fewer iterations always win on noisy hardware","Config B with three iterations, to push the probability higher","Config B with two iterations: 0.80 x 0.945 + 0.20/8 is about 0.78, against 0.88 x 0.781 + 0.12/8, about 0.70, for one iteration. Cutting iterations pays only when noise dominates, as in config A (0.38 for k = 1 versus 0.25 for k = 2). Fix the layout first","Config A with 100,000 shots, since more shots average the noise away"], answer:2, why:"Fewer iterations trade a lower ideal probability for a shorter circuit. That wins when F is small and loses when F is high. Three iterations overshoot: sin^2(7 theta) is about 0.33 even on a perfect machine. Shots shrink the error bar (0.7% to 0.14%), never the bias, and 125 s is a fifth of the monthly allowance."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write a review for your teammate and a run plan for the demo: what the simulator result really means, why the hardware result is low and how the evidence proves it, the transpilation and execution settings you will use, the result you predict before spending QPU time, and how you will present the numbers honestly to students.",
    rubric:["Explains little-endian bitstrings: '011' is q0 = 1, q1 = 1, q2 = 0, the intended target. Warns that 'fixing' the oracle would search for a different item.","Attributes the hardware result to the pinned, scattered layout (37 SWAPs, 141 CZ, depth 488, about 41 us) and to the low T2 of qubits chosen by T1, and shows the calibration model predicting 0.25 against a measured 0.27.","Rejects 100,000 shots: the standard error shrinks from about 0.7% to 0.14% around the same biased 27%, and it spends about 2 of the 10 QPU minutes. Dismisses qubit 113 as unused.","Chooses run settings: no pinned layout (or a connected low-error path chosen from calibration), optimization_level 3 with a fixed seed, CZ count and depth checked before submitting, dynamical decoupling on, a rehearsal on a fake backend or noise model, and 4,000 shots.","Computes the expected success for config B: about 0.78 for k = 2 and 0.70 for k = 1, and keeps k = 2. Notes that k = 3 overshoots (about 0.33 even ideally).","Plans an honest presentation: shows the ideal 94.5%, the 12.5% random-guess baseline, the prediction and the measurement side by side, and records job IDs and QPU seconds."],
    reference:"The simulator is right and so is the oracle. Qiskit prints counts with qubit 0 as the rightmost character, so '011' means q2 = 0, q1 = 1, q0 = 1: exactly the item written as 110 in the notebook. Its 94.5% is sin^2(5 theta), theta = arcsin(1/sqrt 8). 'Fixing' the oracle to mark 011 would make the simulator print '110' while searching for a different item. On hardware the right string came back 27% of the time, not 8.7%, and the layout, not the chip, explains it. Pinning qubits 0, 47 and 121 because they had the best T1 scattered the circuit across the lattice: 37 SWAPs, 141 CZ gates, depth 488, about 41 us, on qubits whose T2 is only 71 to 102 us. The calibration model predicts 0.25 and we measured 0.27: the hardware is doing what its numbers say. Qubit 113 is unused. 100,000 shots would only shrink the error bar from 0.7% to 0.14% around the same biased 27%, and spend about 2 of our 10 QPU minutes. Plan: no pinned layout, optimization_level 3 with a fixed seed, which lands on the connected path 62-63-64 with 32 CZ and about 9 us. Check the counts first, enable dynamical decoupling, rehearse on the FakeKingston noise model, and run 4,000 shots. Predicted success: 0.78 with two iterations against 0.70 with one, so keep two; three would overshoot to 0.33. Show students the ideal, the 12.5% guessing baseline, the prediction and the measurement side by side.",
    alternatives:["Choose the layout by hand: score every connected three-qubit path by CZ error, readout error and T2 from today's calibration, then pass the best one as initial_layout. Equally valid, provided you still check the transpiled CZ count before running.","Add measurement twirling, or correct the histogram with a readout calibration matrix for display. This helps a little, but readout is not the dominant error here, and any mitigation must be labeled as such in the demo. Partly right.","Run on a newer, lower-error backend if your plan has access to one. It helps, but without fixing the layout the SWAP overhead still dominates. Partly right."]},
  hints:["Which end of a Qiskit bitstring is qubit 0? Read '011' with that in mind before touching the oracle.","Compare the two transpiled circuits' CZ count, depth and duration, and look at T2, not just T1, for the pinned qubits.","The answer was right all along (little-endian order), and the pinned, scattered layout cost 37 SWAPs. Let the transpiler pick a connected low-error path at level 3, keep two iterations (about 0.78 predicted) and 4,000 shots."] },
{ id:"fcp42", kind:"case", title:"The ranch link that went quiet in June", level:4, domains:["p42","p10"],
  context:"Your company set up a LoRa link for a ranch client: Meshtastic firmware on the US 902-928 MHz band with the LongFast preset, Part 15 hardware, no license needed. A tank-level sensor at a remote well sends a reading every 5 minutes to a gateway node at the barn, 6.2 km away, which forwards it to the ranch's dashboard. Commissioning in March was clean, with under 1% packet loss. Since June the dashboard has had gaps, and this month more than half the readings are missing. In early June the client's electrician moved the gateway node from the mast top into the barn office so it could sit on mains power next to the router. The client asks whether they need a stronger radio, and the dashboard keeps flagging the sensor's battery as low.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Gateway packet statistics, sensor !a3f0 to gateway !7c12 (288 readings a day)", text:"period            rx/sent    loss    RSSI avg   SNR avg    note\n2026-03-12..03-18  2009/2016  0.3%    -112 dBm   +1.5 dB    commissioning, radio at mast top\n2026-05-01..05-31  8840/8928  1.0%    -116 dBm   -2.0 dB    leaf-out on the creek trees\n2026-06-04..06-30  5642/7776  27.4%   -128 dBm   -14.1 dB   after the June 3 move\n2026-07-01..07-31  4687/8928  47.5%   -130 dBm   -16.0 dB\n2026-10-01..10-25  3311/7200  54.0%   -130 dBm   -16.2 dB\n\nLongFast = SF11, 250 kHz: demodulation floor about -17.5 dB SNR\nreceiver noise floor in 250 kHz (kTB + 6 dB noise figure): about -114 dBm"},
    {label:"Site notes and the electrician's work order", text:"Commissioning (Mar 12): gateway radio in a weatherproof box at the top of a 9 m mast, 6 dBi omni,\n  1 m LMR-240 jumper (0.3 dB). Sensor: 22 dBm SX1262 radio, 3 dBi whip 2 m above ground, 0.3 dB jumper.\nPath: 6.2 km. A creek tree line at 2.9 km from the sensor stands 12-14 m tall; the straight line\n  between the antennas passes about 5 m above the ground there.\n\nWork order, Jun 3: \"Moved Meshtastic box into the office by the router. Ran 82 ft RG-58/U from the mast\n  antenna through the wall, N-to-SMA adapter at the radio (0.5 dB). SWR checked at the radio end: 1.2:1. All good.\"\n\nCoax loss at 915 MHz per 100 ft (manufacturer typicals): RG-58/U ~16 dB | LMR-240 ~7.6 dB | LMR-400 ~3.9 dB"},
    {label:"Sensor telemetry (from the packets that did arrive) and the battery alert", text:"dashboard: \"LOW BATTERY: tank-sensor !a3f0 3.42 V\" (threshold 3.50 V), raised daily around 06:10\n\nOct 24   batt_V  solar_mA  tx_counter\n06:10    3.42    0         101,447\n10:00    3.88    210       101,493\n14:00    4.12    180       101,541\n22:00    3.71    0         101,637\n\nloss by hour of day, Oct 1-25:  00-06 h 53.8% | 06-12 h 54.6% | 12-18 h 53.5% | 18-24 h 54.1%\ntx_counter advances 12 per hour, every hour"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp42q1", q:"What explains the June jump from about 1% to 27% loss?", options:["Summer foliage on the creek tree line","The sensor battery sagging under transmit load","The 82 ft RG-58 run: about 16 dB per 100 ft at 915 MHz is about 13 dB, plus the adapter, so roughly 13 dB more loss than the 1 m jumper, or 95% of the received power. RSSI fell by about that much (-116 to -128 dBm), leaving SNR a few dB above the -17.5 dB floor, so ordinary fading now drops packets","Interference from the router's Wi-Fi in the office"], answer:2, why:"The step lines up with the work order, not with the season: leaf-out cost about 4 dB from March to May and only reached 1% loss. Wi-Fi is at 2.4 and 5 GHz, and SNR still tracks RSSI against the same -114 dBm floor, so the receiver is limited by weak signal, not by added noise."},
    {id:"fcp42q2", q:"The electrician measured SWR 1.2:1 at the radio end. What does that prove?", options:["That the antenna and the cable are fine","Very little: the reflected wave is attenuated twice by the line, so 13 dB of cable loss adds about 26 dB of return loss. Even an open or shorted antenna would read about 1.1:1 at the radio end. Measure at the antenna end, or measure the cable's loss directly","That the cable loss must be under 1 dB","That the antenna is mismatched, because a perfect system reads 1.0:1"], answer:1, why:"With total reflection at the far end, the reflection arriving back at the radio is 10^(-26.6/20), about 0.047, which is an SWR of about 1.10. A lossy line looks like a good dummy load. That is why a low SWR at the bottom of a long, thin coax run is a warning sign, not a reassurance."},
    {id:"fcp42q3", q:"Which plan restores a reliable link and stays legal?", options:["Add a 2 W amplifier at the sensor so it can be heard over the loss","Switch every node to a slower preset for a lower SNR floor","Replace the RG-58 with 82 ft of LMR-240","Put the radio back at the antenna, in a weatherproof box on the mast powered over an outdoor-rated USB or PoE run, with data back to the office over Wi-Fi or Ethernet. At minimum, replace the run with LMR-400 (about 3.2 dB plus the adapter). Then improve the sensor antenna's height toward Fresnel clearance and re-measure, keeping the certified radio and antenna combination"], answer:3, why:"Putting the radio at the mast removes the 13 dB at its source. An amplifier on a Part 15 device moves it outside its certification and drains the battery. A slower preset buys about 2.5 dB of floor at the cost of airtime across the whole mesh. LMR-240 still wastes about 6 dB."},
    {id:"fcp42q4", q:"How should you treat the daily LOW BATTERY alert?", options:["As a dawn dip on a solar-charged cell that recovers to 4.1 V by afternoon. The node's tx_counter advances 12 an hour around the clock and loss is about 54% in every quarter of the day, so the sensor transmits on schedule and the packets are lost on the path. Adjust the alert threshold and move on","As the cause: a weak battery lowers transmit power, so packets do not arrive","As proof that the solar panel failed","As a reason to lengthen the reporting interval to save power"], answer:0, why:"If the battery caused the losses, they would cluster before dawn and the transmit counter would stall; neither happens. A lithium cell resting at 3.4 V after a night without sun, then charging at 180-210 mA, is a normal daily cycle. The alert threshold is simply set above the overnight minimum."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the service report for the ranch client: the link budget then and now, what caused the loss, why the battery alert and the SWR reading are misleading, the fix you recommend with its expected margin, and how you will verify the link afterwards.",
    rubric:["Computes FSPL of about 107.5 dB and a free-space prediction of about -77 dBm, recognizes about 35 dB of excess loss at commissioning (the tree line blocks the line of sight and the 22.5 m first Fresnel zone), and states the original margin (about 19 dB of SNR above the -17.5 dB floor).","Attributes the June step to about 13 dB of RG-58 loss at 915 MHz (82 ft at about 16 dB per 100 ft, plus the adapter), matching the RSSI drop, with foliage adding a few dB, and shows the link now sits 1-2 dB above the SNR floor.","Explains why an SWR of 1.2:1 at the radio end means nothing through a lossy line (return loss doubled; even an open antenna reads about 1.1:1).","Dismisses the battery alert with the tx_counter and the flat loss-by-hour figures.","Recommends moving the radio to the mast (or LMR-400 at minimum) and raising the sensor antenna, staying within Part 15 with the certified radio and antenna, and predicts the recovered margin (about 13 dB back, summer SNR near -3 dB, about 14 dB of margin).","Verifies with a week of loss, RSSI and SNR logging (loss under 2%), an antenna-end SWR or cable-loss measurement, and a seasonal re-check."],
    reference:"Free-space loss over 6.2 km at 915 MHz is 20 log 6.2 + 20 log 915 + 32.44 = 107.5 dB. With 22 dBm, 3 and 6 dBi antennas and 0.6 dB of jumpers, free space predicts about -77 dBm. Commissioning measured -112 dBm: the creek tree line, above the line of sight and deep in the 22.5 m first Fresnel zone, already cost 35 dB. The link worked, but with an SNR of +1.5 dB against LongFast's -17.5 dB floor it had only about 19 dB of margin. Leaf-out took 4 dB by May; the June move took the rest. 82 ft of RG-58 at about 16 dB per 100 ft is 13.1 dB, plus the adapter, so 95% of the signal now heats the cable. RSSI fell to about -130 dBm and SNR to -16 dB, just above the floor, where ordinary fading drops half the packets. The 1.2:1 SWR reading proves nothing: the reflection crosses the lossy cable twice, so even a broken antenna would read about 1.1:1. The battery alert is a dawn dip on a solar-charged cell; the tx counter never stalls and loss is 54% at every hour. Fix: put the radio back in a weatherproof box at the mast, with Wi-Fi back to the router. That recovers about 13 dB: summer SNR near -3 dB, a 14 dB margin, more if the sensor antenna is raised. Verify with a week of logs showing loss under 2%, then re-check in spring.",
    alternatives:["Replace the run with LMR-400 and keep the radio indoors. That costs about 3.2 dB plus the adapter instead of 13.6 dB, recovering about 10 dB. Acceptable if a mast-top box is impractical, but it leaves a few dB on the table.","Raise the sensor antenna on a 6-8 m pole to clear more of the Fresnel zone. This reduces the base excess loss and helps even before the cable fix. Best combined with it.","Move the whole mesh to a slower preset for a lower SNR floor. This buys a couple of dB at the cost of airtime, and every node must change. It does not address the 13 dB being thrown away. Partly right."]},
  hints:["Compare RSSI and SNR before and after the June 3 work order. How many dB did the change cost, and where did they go?","Look up coax loss at 915 MHz, not at VHF, and think about what a reflection travels through before an SWR meter at the radio sees it.","82 ft of RG-58 throws away about 13 dB, leaving SNR a dB or two above LongFast's -17.5 dB floor. Move the radio back to the mast (or use LMR-400), keep the certified hardware, ignore the dawn battery dip, and log a week to verify."] }
,
{ id:"fcp43", kind:"case", title:"Nine Macs that would not patch", level:3, domains:["p43","p3"],
  context:"Your business manages 38 Macs for a small engineering firm through an MDM. Twenty-six were bought through Apple Business Manager and enrolled automatically. The other twelve, older Apple silicon MacBook Pros, were enrolled by hand last year when the firm left its previous IT provider: each user installed the enrollment profile and approved it. Two problems landed this week. A declarative software-update enforcement for macOS Tahoe 26.7.1 (deadline Oct 23, 18:00) installed everywhere except nine of those twelve, which show the update as downloaded but never install it. And a new hire, J. Lopez, whose account your onboarding script created on one of the nine, cannot unlock the Mac at the FileVault screen after a restart; an admin has to log in first. The firm's insurer requires security patches within 14 days of release.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"MDM inventory and enforcement status, Oct 26 09:00", text:"Declaration: software-update enforcement, target 26.7.1, deadline 2026-10-23 18:00 local\ninstalled 26.7.1: 29     not installed: 9     offline > 7 days: 0\n\ngroup                         count  enrollment       supervised  bootstrap token escrowed  26.7.1\nADE Macs                      26     Automated (ADE)  yes         yes (26)                  26 installed\nhand-enrolled, updated         3     User Approved    no          yes (3)                   3 installed\nhand-enrolled, not updated     9     User Approved    no          no  (9)                   9 \"downloaded, awaiting authorization\"\n\nthe nine: MBP-ENG-02, -03, -05, -06, -07, -08, -09, -11, -12 (M1 Pro and M2 Pro, macOS 26.6.1)"},
    {label:"Terminal on MBP-ENG-07 (J. Lopez's Mac)", text:"$ sudo profiles status -type bootstraptoken\nprofiles: Bootstrap Token supported on server: YES\nprofiles: Bootstrap Token escrowed to server: NO\n\n$ profiles status -type enrollment\nEnrolled via DEP: No\nMDM enrollment: Yes (User Approved)\n\n$ sudo fdesetup status\nFileVault is On.\n$ sudo fdesetup list\nitadmin,85C3B3F4-6E1D-4C8A-9A51-3F0E2B7D44C1\n\n$ sysadminctl -secureTokenStatus jlopez\n2026-10-26 09:14:22.871 sysadminctl[2214:31877] Secure token is DISABLED for user jlopez\n$ sysadminctl -secureTokenStatus itadmin\n2026-10-26 09:14:30.105 sysadminctl[2219:31901] Secure token is ENABLED for user itadmin"},
    {label:"onboard_user.sh (run as root by the MDM, Oct 19)", text:"#!/bin/zsh\n# parameters from the MDM policy: $4 short name, $5 full name, $6 temporary password\nNEWUSER=\"$4\"; FULLNAME=\"$5\"; TEMPPW=\"$6\"\nsysadminctl -addUser \"$NEWUSER\" -fullName \"$FULLNAME\" -password \"$TEMPPW\" -home \"/Users/$NEWUSER\"\ncreatehomedir -c -u \"$NEWUSER\" >/dev/null\ndseditgroup -o edit -a \"$NEWUSER\" -t user staff\necho \"created $NEWUSER\"\n# policy log: \"created jlopez\"  exit 0"},
    {label:"Unified log and softwareupdate on MBP-ENG-07", text:"$ log show --last 30m --style compact | grep -c 'Sandbox: .* deny'\n4127\n$ log show --last 30m --style compact | grep 'Sandbox' | head -2\n2026-10-26 09:20:41.112 E  kernel[0:1a2f] Sandbox: mds_stores(412) deny(1) file-read-data /Users/jlopez/Library/Containers/...\n2026-10-26 09:20:41.130 E  kernel[0:1a2f] Sandbox: com.apple.WebKit.Networking(2210) deny(1) mach-lookup com.apple.coreservices...\n\n$ softwareupdate --list\nSoftware Update found the following new or updated software:\n* Label: macOS Tahoe 26.7.1\n\tTitle: macOS Tahoe 26.7.1, Version: 26.7.1, Recommended: YES, Action: restart,"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp43q1", q:"What do the nine failed Macs have that the three successful hand-enrolled Macs do not?", options:["No bootstrap token escrowed to the MDM. On Apple silicon the MDM needs it to authorize an update without a volume owner typing a password, so the enforced install stalls at 'awaiting authorization'","They are unsupervised, and software-update enforcement works only on supervised Macs","They run a macOS version that cannot be updated through MDM","Their MDM push certificate expired"], answer:0, why:"The three hand-enrolled Macs that updated are also unsupervised and User Approved, so supervision and enrollment type are not the discriminator; bootstrap token escrow is the one column that splits the groups exactly. The nine still check in and report status, which rules out a push-certificate problem, and 26.6.1 to 26.7.1 is an ordinary minor update."},
    {id:"fcp43q2", q:"Why can't J. Lopez unlock FileVault at startup?", options:["The MDM's FileVault profile turned FileVault off for new accounts","Her password is too short for FileVault","Only secure-token holders are FileVault-enabled users. The script created her account with sysadminctl and no secure-token admin's credentials, so she has no token, and with no bootstrap token escrowed nothing granted one later. fdesetup list shows only itadmin","The FileVault recovery key was never escrowed, so new users cannot be added"], answer:2, why:"FileVault is On, and the unlock list is the set of secure-token holders shown by fdesetup list. The personal recovery key is a different secret for help-desk unlocks; escrowing it would not add her to the unlock list. A token comes from an existing holder (sysadminctl with -adminUser/-adminPassword) or, in managed fleets, through the bootstrap token."},
    {id:"fcp43q3", q:"Which remediation is correct and safe?", options:["Erase and re-enroll all nine Macs through Automated Device Enrollment this week","Turn FileVault off on the nine Macs so updates and new users stop needing tokens","Give jlopez a token with sysadminctl -secureTokenOn, using her own password as the admin credential","On each of the nine, have a secure-token admin (itadmin) run sudo profiles install -type bootstraptoken and confirm 'escrowed to server: YES'. Then grant jlopez a token with sysadminctl -secureTokenOn jlopez, using itadmin as -adminUser, confirm fdesetup list shows her, and re-run the update enforcement with a new deadline"], answer:3, why:"Escrowing the token is non-destructive and fixes both MDM functions at once. Granting a token always requires an existing holder's credentials, so her own password cannot do it. Erasing and moving to ADE is the right long-term home for these Macs, but it is disruptive and not needed to meet this deadline. Turning off encryption would trade a patching gap for an insurance and breach problem."},
    {id:"fcp43q4", q:"How should you treat the 4,127 Sandbox deny lines in the unified log?", options:["As the cause: the sandbox is blocking softwareupdate","As routine noise: healthy Macs log thousands of sandbox denials an hour, none of these involve softwareupdate or the MDM client, and the failure is fully explained by the token status on exactly the nine Macs","As a privacy-permission (TCC) problem that needs a PPPC profile for softwareupdate","As a reason to reset NVRAM and reinstall macOS"], answer:1, why:"Sandbox denials are processes probing resources their profile does not allow, and they are constant on every Mac. A useful log search starts from the failing subsystem (softwareupdate, mdmclient) and the time of the failure, not from the noisiest error count. Reinstalling would not escrow a bootstrap token."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the remediation report for the firm's office manager, plus your own runbook steps: the root cause of both symptoms, the fix for the nine Macs and for J. Lopez, how you will verify it across the fleet, and the onboarding and enrollment changes that prevent a repeat.",
    rubric:["Links both symptoms to one cause: no bootstrap token escrowed on nine User Approved Apple silicon Macs (profiles status shows NO; the three hand-enrolled Macs that updated show YES), so the MDM cannot authorize updates or grant secure tokens.","Explains secure tokens and volume ownership: jlopez was created by sysadminctl without a token holder's credentials, and fdesetup list shows only itadmin, so she is not a FileVault-enabled user. Distinguishes the bootstrap token from the FileVault recovery key.","Fixes safely: escrows the bootstrap token with sudo profiles install -type bootstraptoken authenticated by itadmin, and grants jlopez a token with sysadminctl -secureTokenOn using itadmin's credentials. Does not disable FileVault or erase Macs as the first step.","Verifies: bootstrap token escrowed on all 38 (an MDM smart group or report at zero exceptions), fdesetup list includes jlopez, 26.7.1 installs after a new deadline, and compliance is reported against the insurer's 14 days.","Prevents: the onboarding script passes -adminUser and -adminPassword (or accounts are created through MDM or Platform SSO with the bootstrap token present) and checks the token afterwards; an alert on 'bootstrap token not escrowed'; and a plan to move the twelve Macs into Apple Business Manager and ADE at their next erase.","Dismisses the sandbox log noise with a reason."],
    reference:"Both symptoms have one cause: nine of the twelve hand-enrolled Apple silicon Macs never escrowed a bootstrap token. profiles status reports 'escrowed to server: NO' on MBP-ENG-07, and the MDM inventory shows the same for exactly the nine Macs that failed. The three hand-enrolled Macs that updated show YES, so enrollment type alone does not decide it. On Apple silicon, the MDM uses the bootstrap token to authorize an update without a volume owner typing a password, so the enforced 26.7.1 install sits downloaded and waiting for authorization. It also uses the token to give secure tokens to accounts created later. Our onboarding script created jlopez with sysadminctl but no secure-token admin's credentials, so she has no token. fdesetup list shows only itadmin, which is why she cannot unlock FileVault at boot. That is separate from the FileVault recovery key, and the 4,127 sandbox denials are normal macOS noise. Fix, per Mac: itadmin, who holds a secure token, runs sudo profiles install -type bootstraptoken, and we confirm 'escrowed to server: YES'. On MBP-ENG-07 we also run sysadminctl -secureTokenOn jlopez with itadmin as -adminUser and confirm fdesetup list shows her. Then we re-run the enforcement with a deadline 48 hours out. Verify: the smart group 'bootstrap token not escrowed' at zero across all 38, and every Mac on 26.7.1 inside the insurer's 14 days. Prevent: fix the onboarding script, alert on that smart group, and move these twelve Macs into Apple Business Manager and ADE at their next erase.",
    alternatives:["Add the twelve Macs to Apple Business Manager with Apple Configurator and re-enroll them through ADE. This gives supervision and automatic bootstrap escrow, but each Mac must be erased, so schedule it for a planned refresh rather than doing it to hit this deadline.","Have itadmin log in once at the login window on each of the nine, since a secure-token user's login can trigger escrow when the MDM supports it. Less deterministic than the explicit profiles command, and it still needs the same verification. Partly right.","Let each user install 26.7.1 with their own password. This gets the patch in on Macs whose users hold tokens, but it does nothing for jlopez and leaves the fleet unable to enforce the next deadline. Partly right."]},
  hints:["Compare the nine failed Macs with the three hand-enrolled Macs that did update. Which inventory column differs?","On Apple silicon, what does the MDM need in order to authorize an update or give a secure token to a new account? Check profiles status and fdesetup list on MBP-ENG-07.","No bootstrap token is escrowed, and jlopez was created without a secure token. Have itadmin run sudo profiles install -type bootstraptoken, grant jlopez a token with sysadminctl -secureTokenOn, verify, re-run the deadline and fix the onboarding script."] },
{ id:"fcp44", kind:"case", title:"A prettier form that fewer people finish", level:4, domains:["p44","p5"],
  context:"Your company redesigned the online service-request form for a client, a regional fiber ISP; customers use it to report outages and book repair visits. The redesign launched Oct 5. It has a custom styled dropdown for the problem type, placeholder text instead of labels, inline validation shown as red borders, and an availability grid where customers drag across the hours they can be home. Since launch, completed requests are down and call-center volume is up 18%. The ISP's compliance lead has also forwarded a formal complaint from a blind customer who could not submit the form with a screen reader. The client's marketing lead says the form 'looks great' and thinks the drop is seasonal. You pulled the analytics, ran an audit and tested the form with six people. Write the review.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Form funnel, three weeks before vs three weeks after launch", text:"step                          Sep 14 - Oct 4 (old)   Oct 5 - Oct 25 (new)\nform viewed                   9,812                  9,640\nstarted (first field)         6,430  (65.5%)         6,388  (66.3%)\nreached availability step     5,690  (88.5%)         5,402  (84.6%)\nsubmitted                     5,121  (90.0%)         3,618  (67.0%)\ncompletion (submitted/started) 79.6%                  56.6%\n  phone (72% of starts)       78.2%                  49.8%\n  desktop                     83.1%                  74.9%\ncall-center 'repair booking' calls per week: 1,140 -> 1,345"},
    {label:"Accessibility audit: axe scan, then manual keyboard and screen-reader testing", text:"axe-core scan of /request: 2 violations\n  serious   color-contrast  3 nodes: .brand-wordmark 2.9:1 (#7FA7D9 on #FFFFFF); .hint (2 nodes) 3.8:1\n  moderate  region          1 node: some page content is not contained by landmarks\n  passes include: label (placeholder accepted as an accessible name), button-name, html-has-lang\n\nmanual test (keyboard only; NVDA + Firefox; TalkBack + Chrome on Android)\n1 problem type: not reachable with Tab; NVDA reads \"clickable, Select a problem\"; no role, no expanded state; arrows do nothing\n2 text fields: placeholder is the only label and disappears once typing starts\n3 errors: red border on blur, no text; nothing announced; Submit stays disabled with no explanation\n4 availability: drag across hour cells only; no tap-per-slot or keyboard alternative; cells are 18 x 18 CSS px\n5 focus: no visible focus indicator anywhere"},
    {label:"Moderated usability test, 6 participants", text:"task: \"Your internet is down. Report it and book a repair visit for Thursday afternoon.\"\nP1  phone, 34     success 3:40   dragged the grid three times: \"it keeps selecting the wrong hours\"\nP2  phone, 68     fail           scrolled the page instead of dragging; gave up at availability\nP3  desktop, 41   success 2:10\nP4  phone, 52, blind, TalkBack   fail   could not open the problem-type dropdown\nP5  phone, 71     fail           typed phone as 210-555-0147; red border only; \"Submit is grey, I don't know what's wrong\"\nP6  desktop, 29   success 1:55\ntask success 3/6; SUS mean 52.5 (old form, last spring: 74)"},
    {label:"Markup excerpt and the marketing lead's note", text:"<div class=\"dd\" onclick=\"openMenu()\">Select a problem</div>\n<ul class=\"dd-menu\" hidden><li onclick=\"pick('outage')\">No internet</li> ...</ul>\n<input id=\"acct\" placeholder=\"Account number\">\n<input id=\"phone\" placeholder=\"Phone\" pattern=\"[0-9]{10}\">\n<div class=\"slots\" onpointerdown=\"startDrag(event)\"> ... 18px cells ... </div>\n<button id=\"submit\" disabled>Submit</button>\n<style> *:focus { outline: none } input:invalid { border: 2px solid #D93025 } </style>\n\nmarketing: \"The form looks great and matches the brand. The drop is probably seasonal, since September storms\ninflated the old numbers. Can we just make the Submit button bigger and brighter?\""}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp44q1", q:"Where did the redesign lose customers, and how do you know it is not seasonal?", options:["At the start: fewer people begin the form because the design looks unfamiliar","Mostly at the last two steps, and on phones. Views and start rates are unchanged (65.5% versus 66.3%), but availability-to-submit fell from 90.0% to 67.0% and phone completion from 78.2% to 49.8%. A seasonal dip would lower views and starts, not one step on one device class","Evenly across all steps, which points to slower page loads","Only on desktop"], answer:1, why:"Demand (views) and intent (starts) held steady, so the 'storm season' theory fails on the marketing lead's own data. A step-specific, device-specific drop points at interaction design: exactly the steps that changed are the ones that lose people, and phones are where dragging and tiny targets hurt most."},
    {id:"fcp44q2", q:"Which mapping of the findings to WCAG 2.2 success criteria is right?", options:["Only 1.4.3 Contrast is violated, because that is all axe reported","The dropdown fails 1.1.1 Non-text Content and the drag grid fails 1.4.10 Reflow","Dropdown: 2.1.1 Keyboard and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value. Vanishing placeholder labels: 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions. Red-border-only errors: 3.3.1 Error Identification and 1.4.1 Use of Color. Drag-only availability: 2.5.7 Dragging Movements, and 18 px cells fail 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum). outline: none: 2.4.7 Focus Visible","None, because axe passes the label rule and found no keyboard violations"], answer:2, why:"2.5.7 and 2.5.8 are new in WCAG 2.2 at level AA and fit this grid exactly: dragging needs a single-pointer alternative, and targets need at least 24 x 24 CSS px. A div with onclick has no role, no state and no keyboard handling, which is what 4.1.2 and 2.1.1 cover. Automated tools cannot judge most of this."},
    {id:"fcp44q3", q:"How should you treat axe's 'serious' contrast finding on .brand-wordmark (2.9:1)?", options:["As the red herring: text that is part of a logo or brand name is exempt from 1.4.3, so the wordmark needs no change. The .hint text at 3.8:1 is a real failure (body text needs 4.5:1) but minor next to the blockers, and axe's silence about the dropdown and the drag grid shows why automated scans must be paired with manual testing","As the top priority, because it is the lowest contrast ratio on the page","As proof that the whole brand palette fails WCAG","As the reason the screen-reader user could not submit"], answer:0, why:"WCAG 1.4.3 explicitly exempts logotypes. A screen reader does not depend on color contrast, so this finding cannot explain P4's failure. Automated scanners typically find a minority of real accessibility issues; here they accepted placeholder text as a label and could not see that the dropdown was a mouse-only div."},
    {id:"fcp44q4", q:"Only 3 of 6 test participants succeeded. What can you responsibly conclude from the test?", options:["Nothing: six participants is too few to conclude anything","That exactly 50% of real customers fail the new form","That the old form is better, because a SUS of 74 beats 52.5 with statistical significance","The failures are strong qualitative evidence, because each has a clear, reproducible cause (the drag grid, the inaccessible dropdown, the silent phone-format error). The success rate itself is imprecise (adjusted-Wald 95% interval of about 19% to 81%), so use the funnel's thousands of sessions for the size of the effect and the test for its causes"], answer:3, why:"Small moderated tests are for finding problems, not for estimating rates. Adjusted Wald: (3 + 1.92) / (6 + 3.84) = 0.50, with a margin of about 1.96 x sqrt(0.25 / 9.84), roughly 0.31. The SUS comparison crosses different participants and seasons, so it supports the story but proves nothing on its own."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the design review for the ISP's product owner and compliance lead: what broke and the evidence, the WCAG 2.2 failures with their criteria, the fixes in priority order (with the specific HTML, ARIA and design changes), how you will measure the result, and your answer to the marketing lead.",
    rubric:["Uses the funnel to locate the loss (the availability and submit steps; phone completion from 78.2% to 49.8%) and refutes the seasonal explanation with the unchanged view and start rates.","Maps each defect to WCAG 2.2 criteria (2.1.1 and 4.1.2 for the dropdown; 3.3.2 for placeholders; 3.3.1 and 1.4.1 for errors; 2.5.7 and 2.5.8 for the drag grid; 2.4.7 for focus) and notes the legal exposure that the formal complaint creates.","Prioritizes concrete fixes: a native select (first rule of ARIA); tap-to-pick time slots as radio buttons at least 24 px (ideally 44 px); persistent visible labels; text error messages linked with aria-describedby and announced; phone numbers accepted in any common format; Submit kept enabled, with errors explained on submit; a visible focus style restored.","Treats the wordmark contrast as exempt, fixes the 3.8:1 hint text, and explains why axe missed the blockers.","Measures the result: a retest with five or six users including assistive-technology users (task success, SUS), keyboard, NVDA and TalkBack checks, and funnel completion back near the old 79.6% overall and 78% on phones, with call-center volume tracked.","Answers marketing with evidence: the visual style can stay, and a bigger Submit button does not fix a disabled button with silent errors."],
    reference:"The redesign did not lose interest; it lost people at the end. Views and start rates are unchanged (65.5% versus 66.3%), which rules out a seasonal dip. The losses sit at the availability step and at submit: availability-to-submit fell from 90.0% to 67.0%, and phone completion from 78.2% to 49.8%. The usability test shows why. The drag-across-hours grid fails 2.5.7 Dragging Movements, its 18 px cells fail 2.5.8 Target Size, and two older participants could not use it. The problem-type dropdown is a div with onclick: it cannot be reached by keyboard and exposes no role or state, failing 2.1.1 and 4.1.2, so the TalkBack user could not start. Errors are a red border only, failing 3.3.1 and 1.4.1, and a phone number typed with dashes is silently rejected while Submit stays grey. Placeholder-only labels vanish while typing (3.3.2), and outline: none removes focus (2.4.7). After a formal complaint, these are legal exposure, not polish. Fix in this order: a native select; time slots as large radio buttons (at least 44 px); visible labels; text errors linked with aria-describedby; phone numbers in any format; Submit always enabled, with errors explained on submit; a visible focus style. The wordmark's 2.9:1 is exempt as a logotype, but the 3.8:1 hint text needs 4.5:1. axe missed the blockers, so manual NVDA and TalkBack testing is part of done. Measure with a six-person retest including assistive-technology users, targeting completion near 80% on every device. To marketing: keep the look; a bigger grey button fixes nothing.",
    alternatives:["Build a custom combobox that follows the ARIA Authoring Practices pattern instead of using a native select. Acceptable if it fully implements roles, states and keyboard support and is tested with screen readers, but it costs far more than a native select for no benefit to users here.","Roll back to the old form while the fixes are built. This stops the losses immediately and is reasonable if the fixes take more than a sprint, though the old form needs its own audit.","Run an A/B test of old against new before changing anything. Unnecessary here: these are known WCAG failures with clear causes, and leaving them live while testing keeps excluding customers. Fix first, then measure. Partly right at best."]},
  hints:["Read the funnel step by step and split it by device. Where does the drop start, and do the view and start numbers support the 'seasonal' theory?","Try the form with only a keyboard and with a screen reader, as the manual audit did. Which interactions have no keyboard or non-drag alternative, and which WCAG 2.2 criteria cover them?","The drag grid, the div dropdown and the silent red-border errors are the blockers (2.5.7, 2.5.8, 2.1.1, 4.1.2, 3.3.1, 1.4.1). Use a native select, tap-to-pick slots, visible labels and text errors; ignore the logotype contrast; retest and watch phone completion."] }
);
